window.__programmeIndexChunk("passages",{"9e165fd162df3cf049297095798bfc575acd547f00bafd8bca2d0be9678a5a2c":{"markdown":"## WR-3. Closability on the entire finite-star domain\n\nBy [GW-1](OA-FLOW-GW.md#oa-flow.gw.1) and [GW-5](OA-FLOW-GW.md#oa-flow.gw.5), \\(\\mathcal A=\\Lambda(N\\cap N^*)\\) is a dense algebra with dense product span. Faithfulness makes \\(\\Lambda\\) injective. Its algebra product, bounded left multiplication and involution are\n\\[\n\\Lambda(x)\\Lambda(y)=\\Lambda(xy),\\quad\nL_{\\Lambda(x)}=\\pi(x),\\quad\nS_0\\Lambda(x)=\\Lambda(x^*)\\qquad(x,y\\in N\\cap N^*).\n\\tag{WR5}\n\\]\nGW supplies the Hilbert adjoint identity. It remains to prove closability of \\(S_0\\), on this complete domain.\n\nFor \\(f,g\\in\\mathcal F\\), \\(y'\\in P'\\), and \\(x\\in N\\cap N^*\\), (WR3) and the linear-first convention give\n\\[\n\\begin{split}\n\\langle S_0\\Lambda(x),t_f^{1/2}y'\\alpha_g\\rangle\n&=\\langle t_f^{1/2}\\Lambda(x^*),y'\\alpha_g\\rangle\\\\\n&=\\langle\\pi(x^*)\\alpha_f,y'\\alpha_g\\rangle\\\\\n&=\\langle\\alpha_f,\\pi(x)y'\\alpha_g\\rangle\\\\\n&=\\langle y'^*\\alpha_f,\\pi(x)\\alpha_g\\rangle\\\\\n&=\\langle t_g^{1/2}y'^*\\alpha_f,\\Lambda(x)\\rangle.\n\\end{split}\n\\tag{WR6}\n\\]\nThus \\(t_f^{1/2}y'\\alpha_g\\) belongs to \\(D(S_0^*)\\), with adjoint value \\(t_g^{1/2}y'^*\\alpha_f\\). [WR-2](OA-FLOW-WR.md#wr-2) makes the span of these vectors dense: for fixed \\(y',g\\), let \\(t_f^{1/2}\\to I\\), and then use density of \\(P'\\alpha_g\\). The conjugate-linear adjoint criterion and graph argument of CI prove that \\(S_0\\) is closable. Write \\(S=\\overline{S_0}\\), \\(F=S^*\\). CI proves both are closed densely defined conjugate-linear involutions, with their full reciprocal adjoint domains.\n\nConsequently \\(\\mathcal A\\) is a left Hilbert algebra. Its represented von Neumann algebra is \\(P\\): GW gives ultraweak density of \\(\\mathfrak m\\subset N\\cap N^*\\) in \\(M\\), and [ST-2](OA-FLOW-ST12.md#oa-flow.st.2) transports this density to \\(P\\). No faithful-state restriction or vector \\(\\Lambda(1)\\) has been introduced.\n\n<a id=\"oa-flow.wr.4\"><\/a><a id=\"wr-4\"><\/a>","html":"<h2>WR-3. Closability on the entire finite-star domain<\/h2>\n<p>By <a href=\"../courses/OA-FLOW/OA-FLOW-GW.html#oa-flow.gw.1\">GW-1<\/a> and <a href=\"../courses/OA-FLOW/OA-FLOW-GW.html#oa-flow.gw.5\">GW-5<\/a>, <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(\\mathcal A=\\Lambda(N\\cap N^*)\\)\">\\(\\mathcal A=\\Lambda(N\\cap N^*)\\)<\/span> is a dense algebra with dense product span. Faithfulness makes <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(\\Lambda\\)\">\\(\\Lambda\\)<\/span> injective. Its algebra product, bounded left multiplication and involution are\n<span class=\"math display\" data-tex=\"\\[\n\\Lambda(x)\\Lambda(y)=\\Lambda(xy),\\quad\nL_{\\Lambda(x)}=\\pi(x),\\quad\nS_0\\Lambda(x)=\\Lambda(x^*)\\qquad(x,y\\in N\\cap N^*).\n\\tag{WR5}\n\\]\">\\[\n\\Lambda(x)\\Lambda(y)=\\Lambda(xy),\\quad\nL_{\\Lambda(x)}=\\pi(x),\\quad\nS_0\\Lambda(x)=\\Lambda(x^*)\\qquad(x,y\\in N\\cap N^*).\n\\tag{WR5}\n\\]<\/span>\nGW supplies the Hilbert adjoint identity. It remains to prove closability of <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(S_0\\)\">\\(S_0\\)<\/span>, on this complete domain.<\/p>\n<p>For <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(f,g\\in\\mathcal F\\)\">\\(f,g\\in\\mathcal F\\)<\/span>, <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(y&#x27;\\in P&#x27;\\)\">\\(y&#x27;\\in P&#x27;\\)<\/span>, and <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(x\\in N\\cap N^*\\)\">\\(x\\in N\\cap N^*\\)<\/span>, (WR3) and the linear-first convention give\n<span class=\"math display\" data-tex=\"\\[\n\\begin{split}\n\\langle S_0\\Lambda(x),t_f^{1/2}y&#x27;\\alpha_g\\rangle\n&amp;=\\langle t_f^{1/2}\\Lambda(x^*),y&#x27;\\alpha_g\\rangle\\\\\n&amp;=\\langle\\pi(x^*)\\alpha_f,y&#x27;\\alpha_g\\rangle\\\\\n&amp;=\\langle\\alpha_f,\\pi(x)y&#x27;\\alpha_g\\rangle\\\\\n&amp;=\\langle y&#x27;^*\\alpha_f,\\pi(x)\\alpha_g\\rangle\\\\\n&amp;=\\langle t_g^{1/2}y&#x27;^*\\alpha_f,\\Lambda(x)\\rangle.\n\\end{split}\n\\tag{WR6}\n\\]\">\\[\n\\begin{split}\n\\langle S_0\\Lambda(x),t_f^{1/2}y&#x27;\\alpha_g\\rangle\n&amp;=\\langle t_f^{1/2}\\Lambda(x^*),y&#x27;\\alpha_g\\rangle\\\\\n&amp;=\\langle\\pi(x^*)\\alpha_f,y&#x27;\\alpha_g\\rangle\\\\\n&amp;=\\langle\\alpha_f,\\pi(x)y&#x27;\\alpha_g\\rangle\\\\\n&amp;=\\langle y&#x27;^*\\alpha_f,\\pi(x)\\alpha_g\\rangle\\\\\n&amp;=\\langle t_g^{1/2}y&#x27;^*\\alpha_f,\\Lambda(x)\\rangle.\n\\end{split}\n\\tag{WR6}\n\\]<\/span>\nThus <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(t_f^{1/2}y&#x27;\\alpha_g\\)\">\\(t_f^{1/2}y&#x27;\\alpha_g\\)<\/span> belongs to <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(D(S_0^*)\\)\">\\(D(S_0^*)\\)<\/span>, with adjoint value <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(t_g^{1/2}y&#x27;^*\\alpha_f\\)\">\\(t_g^{1/2}y&#x27;^*\\alpha_f\\)<\/span>. <a href=\"../courses/OA-FLOW/OA-FLOW-WR.html#wr-2\">WR-2<\/a> makes the span of these vectors dense: for fixed <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(y&#x27;,g\\)\">\\(y&#x27;,g\\)<\/span>, let <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(t_f^{1/2}\\to I\\)\">\\(t_f^{1/2}\\to I\\)<\/span>, and then use density of <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(P&#x27;\\alpha_g\\)\">\\(P&#x27;\\alpha_g\\)<\/span>. The conjugate-linear adjoint criterion and graph argument of CI prove that <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(S_0\\)\">\\(S_0\\)<\/span> is closable. Write <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(S=\\overline{S_0}\\)\">\\(S=\\overline{S_0}\\)<\/span>, <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(F=S^*\\)\">\\(F=S^*\\)<\/span>. CI proves both are closed densely defined conjugate-linear involutions, with their full reciprocal adjoint domains.<\/p>\n<p>Consequently <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(\\mathcal A\\)\">\\(\\mathcal A\\)<\/span> is a left Hilbert algebra. Its represented von Neumann algebra is <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(P\\)\">\\(P\\)<\/span>: GW gives ultraweak density of <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(\\mathfrak m\\subset N\\cap N^*\\)\">\\(\\mathfrak m\\subset N\\cap N^*\\)<\/span> in <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(M\\)\">\\(M\\)<\/span>, and <a href=\"../courses/OA-FLOW/OA-FLOW-ST12.html#oa-flow.st.2\">ST-2<\/a> transports this density to <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(P\\)\">\\(P\\)<\/span>. No faithful-state restriction or vector <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(\\Lambda(1)\\)\">\\(\\Lambda(1)\\)<\/span> has been introduced.<\/p>\n","source":"docs/courses/OA-FLOW/src/OA-FLOW-WR.md","start_offset":7930,"end_offset":9806,"role":"registered_statement_and_proof_passage"},"9e69a3c8d9a0269834f2a1f637fceac3370588347e9e046877ba8bb12a48a8ad":{"markdown":"## QF6. Local vector measurability and finite-measure integration\n\nOn a compact space with finite completed Radon measure, an almost everywhere limit of finite-valued measurable simple functions is continuous after deleting a set of arbitrarily small measure and retaining a compact subset. Here are the details. If \\(s_n\\to v\\) pointwise off a null set, let\n\n<a id=\"equation-qf7\"><\/a>\n\n\\[\n A_{m,N}=\\bigcup_{n\\ge N}\\{\\|s_n-v\\|>1/m\\}.\n \\tag{QF7}\n\\]\n\nFor fixed \\(m\\), these sets decrease to a null set. Finite-measure continuity from SC4 gives \\(N_m\\) with \\(\\mu(A_{m,N_m})<\\epsilon2^{-m-2}\\). Off their union convergence is uniform. For each \\(n\\), finite inner regularity replaces each of the finitely many level sets of \\(s_n\\) by a compact subset, with total loss below \\(\\epsilon2^{-n-2}\\). On their finite union \\(s_n\\) is continuous, since disjoint compact subsets are relatively open there. Intersect these retained sets for all \\(n\\), and use finite inner regularity once more to retain a compact subset of the uniform-convergence set. The total loss can be made less than \\(\\epsilon\\). Uniform convergence on it makes \\(v\\) continuous.\n\nConversely, continuity on compact subsets with omitted measures tending to zero gives, outside a null set, a range contained in a countable union of compact metric images, hence a separable closed subspace. A Borel representative on that range has simple approximants by the countable-ball construction of L24. On a space decomposed into open and closed sigma compact pieces \\(O_i\\) as in QF3, the Borel representative can be constructed without an uncountable union of Borel sets of uncontrolled complexity. Choose compact exhaustions \\(C_{i,n}\\) whose interiors cover each \\(O_i\\). Local measurability gives compact \\(F_{i,n,m}\\subset C_{i,n}\\) on which the field is continuous, with omitted measure below \\(2^{-m}\\). Each \\(F_{n,m}=\\bigcup_iF_{i,n,m}\\) is closed in the whole space, and the restricted field is continuous there, since the pieces \\(O_i\\) are open and closed. Its complement \\(N=X\\setminus\\bigcup_{n,m}F_{n,m}\\) is Borel and null on every compact: in each \\(C_{i,n}\\) its measure is at most \\(2^{-m}\\) for every \\(m\\), and any compact meets only finitely many pieces. Define the field to be zero on \\(N\\), and use its original values on the first \\(F_{n,m}\\) containing a point. This is a countable Borel partition with continuous restrictions, hence a Borel field. In the inner-regular convention \\(N\\) is globally null as well. This proves the needed representative assertion both for scalar locally integrable densities and for the vector fields below. Their local continuity convention and compactwise strong measurability agree, without a globally separable target.\n\nThe Bochner integral construction of L24 also applies to a finite completed measure \\(\\nu\\), not just to Haar measure. Explicitly, for a strongly measurable Banach-valued \\(v\\) with \\(\\int\\|v\\|\\,d\\nu<\\infty\\), truncate its norm and approximate its separable range by a countable \\(1/n\\)-net; truncate the resulting countable partition to finitely many pieces. These simple functions approximate \\(v\\) in \\(L^1(\\nu)\\). Their integrals are Cauchy by the simple-function norm inequality, so completeness gives \\(\\int v\\,d\\nu\\), with \\(\\|\\int v\\|\\le\\int\\|v\\|\\). The same inequality proves independence of approximants and permits every bounded linear map to pass through the integral. This is all the vector integration used below.","html":"<h2>QF6. Local vector measurability and finite-measure integration<\/h2>\n<p>On a compact space with finite completed Radon measure, an almost everywhere limit of finite-valued measurable simple functions is continuous after deleting a set of arbitrarily small measure and retaining a compact subset. Here are the details. If <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(s_n\\to v\\)\">\\(s_n\\to v\\)<\/span> pointwise off a null set, let<\/p>\n<p><span class=\"math display\" data-tex=\"\\[\n A_{m,N}=\\bigcup_{n\\ge N}\\{\\|s_n-v\\|&gt;1/m\\}.\n \\tag{QF7}\n\\]\">\\[\n A_{m,N}=\\bigcup_{n\\ge N}\\{\\|s_n-v\\|&gt;1/m\\}.\n \\tag{QF7}\n\\]<\/span><\/p>\n<p>For fixed <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(m\\)\">\\(m\\)<\/span>, these sets decrease to a null set. Finite-measure continuity from SC4 gives <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(N_m\\)\">\\(N_m\\)<\/span> with <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(\\mu(A_{m,N_m})&lt;\\epsilon2^{-m-2}\\)\">\\(\\mu(A_{m,N_m})&lt;\\epsilon2^{-m-2}\\)<\/span>. Off their union convergence is uniform. For each <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(n\\)\">\\(n\\)<\/span>, finite inner regularity replaces each of the finitely many level sets of <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(s_n\\)\">\\(s_n\\)<\/span> by a compact subset, with total loss below <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(\\epsilon2^{-n-2}\\)\">\\(\\epsilon2^{-n-2}\\)<\/span>. On their finite union <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(s_n\\)\">\\(s_n\\)<\/span> is continuous, since disjoint compact subsets are relatively open there. Intersect these retained sets for all <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(n\\)\">\\(n\\)<\/span>, and use finite inner regularity once more to retain a compact subset of the uniform-convergence set. The total loss can be made less than <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(\\epsilon\\)\">\\(\\epsilon\\)<\/span>. Uniform convergence on it makes <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(v\\)\">\\(v\\)<\/span> continuous.<\/p>\n<p>Conversely, continuity on compact subsets with omitted measures tending to zero gives, outside a null set, a range contained in a countable union of compact metric images, hence a separable closed subspace. A Borel representative on that range has simple approximants by the countable-ball construction of L24. On a space decomposed into open and closed sigma compact pieces <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(O_i\\)\">\\(O_i\\)<\/span> as in QF3, the Borel representative can be constructed without an uncountable union of Borel sets of uncontrolled complexity. Choose compact exhaustions <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(C_{i,n}\\)\">\\(C_{i,n}\\)<\/span> whose interiors cover each <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(O_i\\)\">\\(O_i\\)<\/span>. Local measurability gives compact <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(F_{i,n,m}\\subset C_{i,n}\\)\">\\(F_{i,n,m}\\subset C_{i,n}\\)<\/span> on which the field is continuous, with omitted measure below <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(2^{-m}\\)\">\\(2^{-m}\\)<\/span>. Each <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(F_{n,m}=\\bigcup_iF_{i,n,m}\\)\">\\(F_{n,m}=\\bigcup_iF_{i,n,m}\\)<\/span> is closed in the whole space, and the restricted field is continuous there, since the pieces <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(O_i\\)\">\\(O_i\\)<\/span> are open and closed. Its complement <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(N=X\\setminus\\bigcup_{n,m}F_{n,m}\\)\">\\(N=X\\setminus\\bigcup_{n,m}F_{n,m}\\)<\/span> is Borel and null on every compact: in each <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(C_{i,n}\\)\">\\(C_{i,n}\\)<\/span> its measure is at most <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(2^{-m}\\)\">\\(2^{-m}\\)<\/span> for every <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(m\\)\">\\(m\\)<\/span>, and any compact meets only finitely many pieces. Define the field to be zero on <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(N\\)\">\\(N\\)<\/span>, and use its original values on the first <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(F_{n,m}\\)\">\\(F_{n,m}\\)<\/span> containing a point. This is a countable Borel partition with continuous restrictions, hence a Borel field. In the inner-regular convention <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(N\\)\">\\(N\\)<\/span> is globally null as well. This proves the needed representative assertion both for scalar locally integrable densities and for the vector fields below. Their local continuity convention and compactwise strong measurability agree, without a globally separable target.<\/p>\n<p>The Bochner integral construction of L24 also applies to a finite completed measure <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(\\nu\\)\">\\(\\nu\\)<\/span>, not just to Haar measure. Explicitly, for a strongly measurable Banach-valued <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(v\\)\">\\(v\\)<\/span> with <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(\\int\\|v\\|\\,d\\nu&lt;\\infty\\)\">\\(\\int\\|v\\|\\,d\\nu&lt;\\infty\\)<\/span>, truncate its norm and approximate its separable range by a countable <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(1/n\\)\">\\(1/n\\)<\/span>-net; truncate the resulting countable partition to finitely many pieces. These simple functions approximate <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(v\\)\">\\(v\\)<\/span> in <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(L^1(\\nu)\\)\">\\(L^1(\\nu)\\)<\/span>. Their integrals are Cauchy by the simple-function norm inequality, so completeness gives <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(\\int v\\,d\\nu\\)\">\\(\\int v\\,d\\nu\\)<\/span>, with <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(\\|\\int v\\|\\le\\int\\|v\\|\\)\">\\(\\|\\int v\\|\\le\\int\\|v\\|\\)<\/span>. The same inequality proves independence of approximants and permits every bounded linear map to pass through the integral. This is all the vector integration used below.<\/p>\n","source":"docs/courses/OA-FLOW/src/OA-FLOW-QF.md","start_offset":11342,"end_offset":14804,"role":"registered_statement_and_proof_passage"},"9eed54cd647518605b806995a56e9b3e34398c298d206c96b9c1c748d0dc2574":{"markdown":"## GDA7. A dense GNS test determines the weight\n\nThe following Hilbert-space observation is the mechanism. Let \\(B\\) be a closed positive self-adjoint operator on a Hilbert space \\(H\\), and let \\(\\mathcal E\\subset D(B)\\) be a dense linear subspace on which \\(\\|B\\xi\\|=\\|\\xi\\|\\). Then \\(B=1\\). Indeed, for any \\(\\xi\\in H\\), choose \\(\\xi_n\\in\\mathcal E\\) tending to \\(\\xi\\). Linearity gives\n\\[\n \\|B\\xi_n-B\\xi_m\\|=\\|\\xi_n-\\xi_m\\|,\n\\]\nso \\(B\\xi_n\\) converges. Closedness puts \\(\\xi\\) in \\(D(B)\\) and identifies that limit with \\(B\\xi\\). Thus \\(D(B)=H\\) and \\(B\\) is an isometry. Polarization gives \\(B^*B=1\\); since \\(B=B^*\\geq0\\), uniqueness of the positive square root of \\(1\\), proved in [CF9](OA-FLOW-CF.md#oa-flow.cf.9), gives \\(B=1\\). The approximating sequence is chosen separately for each vector; the Hilbert space need not be separable.\n\n**GNS uniqueness criterion.** Let \\(\\Phi,\\Psi\\) be faithful normal semifinite weights on a von Neumann algebra \\(N\\), with the same modular automorphism group. Suppose a linear subspace \\(\\mathcal C\\subset\\mathfrak n_\\Phi\\) satisfies\n\\[\n \\overline{\\Lambda_\\Phi(\\mathcal C)}=H_\\Phi,\\qquad\n \\Psi(a^*a)=\\Phi(a^*a)\\quad(a\\in\\mathcal C).\n\\]\nThen \\(\\Psi=\\Phi\\) on the entire positive cone. No algebra or multiplication-invariance condition on \\(\\mathcal C\\) is required.\n\n**Central density.** We first derive the density needed for the criterion from the earlier local proofs. If faithful n.s.f. weights \\(\\rho,\\nu\\) have \\((D\\rho:D\\nu)_r=1\\), [BC4](OA-FLOW-BC.md#bc-4) gives the constant off-diagonal orbit \\(\\beta_r^{\\rho,\\nu}(1)=1\\). Its norm-entire extension has values one at \\(-i\\) and \\(-i/2\\). [MA4](OA-FLOW-MA.md#oa-flow.ma.4), equation MA11 with \\(k=1\\), gives both inequalities \\(\\rho(h)\\leq\\nu(h)\\) and \\(\\nu(h)\\leq\\rho(h)\\), for every \\(h\\geq0\\), including infinite values. The BC chain rule also proves injectivity for cocycles with a fixed reference weight.\n\nEquality of the modular groups makes \\(u_r=(D\\Psi:D\\Phi)_r\\) central, by BC4. The group fixes the center: a central self-adjoint multiplier commutes with the full finite-star involution and its adjoint, hence with their positive product and imaginary powers, by [NC4](OA-FLOW-NC.md#oa-flow.nc.4). The cocycle law is consequently \\(u_{r+s}=u_ru_s\\). [RF5](OA-FLOW-RF.md#rf-5) gives the self-adjoint generator \\(A\\), with \\(u_r=e^{irA}\\). Its resolvents and spectral projections commute with every operator commuting with the center, so \\(A\\) is affiliated with the center. The full Borel calculus of [SF](OA-FLOW-SF.md#oa-flow.sf.sb3) gives the positive nonsingular central operator \\(h=e^A\\). The density construction [CZ1–6](OA-FLOW-CZ.md#cz-1) supplies a faithful n.s.f. weight \\(\\Phi_h\\) with cocycle \\(h^{ir}=u_r\\). Fixed-reference injectivity therefore gives\n<a id=\"equation-gda31\"><\/a>\n\\[\n \\Psi(X)=\\Phi_h(X)\n =\\sup_n\\Phi(h_n^{1/2}Xh_n^{1/2}),\\qquad\n h_n=\\min(h,n),\\quad X\\in N_+.\n \\tag{GDA31}\n\\]\nTo compare these cutoffs with CZ's resolvent regularizations, use\n\\(h(1+\\varepsilon h)^{-1}\\leq h_n\\) when \\(n\\geq\\varepsilon^{-1}\\), and\n\\(h_n\\leq(1+\\varepsilon n)h(1+\\varepsilon h)^{-1}\\).\nCentrality makes these positive-operator inequalities valid inside the weight evaluations. Letting \\(n\\) and \\(\\varepsilon^{-1}\\) increase proves equality of the two suprema, including their infinite values.\n\n**Proof of the criterion.** Represent \\(N\\) faithfully on \\(H_\\Phi\\), as in [NF5](OA-FLOW-NF.md#oa-flow.nf.5), and transport the spectral projections of \\(h\\) by this normal representation. Let \\(B=\\pi_\\Phi(h)^{1/2}\\) be the resulting closed positive operator, with its full domain from [SB3–4](OA-FLOW-SF.md#oa-flow.sf.sb3). For \\(a\\in\\mathfrak n_\\Phi\\), centrality and the bounded GNS identity give\n<a id=\"equation-gda32\"><\/a>\n\\[\n \\begin{aligned}\n \\Psi(a^*a)\n &=\\sup_n\\Phi(a^*h_n a)\\\\\n &=\\sup_n\\|\\pi_\\Phi(h_n)^{1/2}\\Lambda_\\Phi(a)\\|^2\n =\\|B\\Lambda_\\Phi(a)\\|^2 .\n \\end{aligned}\n \\tag{GDA32}\n\\]\nThe last expression is interpreted as infinity off \\(D(B)\\); its equality with the supremum is precisely the positive spectral-domain criterion. On \\(\\mathcal E=\\Lambda_\\Phi(\\mathcal C)\\) the hypothesis makes it finite and gives \\(\\|B\\xi\\|=\\|\\xi\\|\\). The dense-space observation proves \\(B=1\\). Faithfulness of \\(\\pi_\\Phi\\), applied to the spectral projections, then gives \\(h=1\\). Equation (GDA31) proves \\(\\Psi=\\Phi\\) on all \\(N_+\\). Only the closed operator is extended from a dense set; no continuity of an unbounded weight on a dense positive subset is assumed. \\(\\square\\)\n\n**Application to the dual weight.** Take \\(\\Phi=\\widehat\\varphi\\), the weight constructed by GDW. Suppose a faithful n.s.f. weight \\(\\Psi\\) has its two modular generator formulas and the same quadratic values at every \\(L_x\\), \\(x\\in\\mathscr B_\\varphi\\). Normality and the two generating families give equality of the modular groups on \\(R\\). Use the linear space\n\\(\\mathcal C=L(\\mathscr D_\\varphi)\\subset L(\\mathscr B_\\varphi)\\).\n[GDW6](OA-FLOW-GDW.md#gdw-6) identifies\n\\(\\Lambda_\\Phi(L_x)=\\eta_\\varphi(x)\\), and [GDW2](OA-FLOW-GDW.md#gdw-2) proves that \\(\\eta_\\varphi(\\mathscr D_\\varphi)\\) is dense in the whole \\(L^2(G,H_\\varphi)\\). The GNS criterion therefore applies and yields\n<a id=\"equation-gda33\"><\/a>\n\\[\n \\Psi=\\widehat\\varphi\\quad\\hbox{on the entire positive cone}.\n \\tag{GDA33}\n\\]\nThe group, algebra and Hilbert space retain their full arbitrary scope. The classical dual-weight characterization appears in Takesaki, [*Theory of Operator Algebras II*, Chapter X, Lemma 1.18, printed p.250](https://doi.org/10.1007/978-3-662-10451-4); the criterion above uses the closed positive density operator on the GNS Hilbert space.\n\n### The closed-graph mechanism\n\n![Central density, the GNS quadratic identity, closed-graph extension and positivity](../assets/gns-weight-uniqueness/gns-weight-uniqueness.png)\n\nThe four boxes display the actual proof maps and domains. The density \\(h\\) is central and nonsingular; \\(B=\\pi_\\Phi(h)^{1/2}\\) acts on \\(H_\\Phi\\), and \\(\\mathcal E=\\Lambda_\\Phi(\\mathcal C)\\) is a linear dense subspace of its domain. Equality (GDA32) makes \\(B|_{\\mathcal E}\\) isometric. For every \\(\\xi\\in H_\\Phi\\), the two coordinates of \\((\\xi_n,B\\xi_n)\\) converge, so the closed graph puts their limit in the graph of \\(B\\). This proves the full domain assertion before positivity forces \\(B=1\\). A general isometry need not be the identity; positivity is the essential final hypothesis. The diagram is a logical schematic of this arbitrary-dimensional argument, not a numerical or finite-dimensional approximation.\n\nThe local proofs used for the central density are MA4, BC4, NC4, RF5 and CZ1–6, as specified above. [Editable SVG](../assets/gns-weight-uniqueness/gns-weight-uniqueness.svg), [exact diagram labels](../assets/gns-weight-uniqueness/data.json), and [reproduction source](../assets/gns-weight-uniqueness/render.py) accompany the figure. The diagram and caption are CC0-1.0 to the extent of rights held; DejaVu font terms remain applicable.\n\n<a id=\"gda-8\"><\/a>","html":"<h2>GDA7. A dense GNS test determines the weight<\/h2>\n<p>The following Hilbert-space observation is the mechanism. Let <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(B\\)\">\\(B\\)<\/span> be a closed positive self-adjoint operator on a Hilbert space <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(H\\)\">\\(H\\)<\/span>, and let <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(\\mathcal E\\subset D(B)\\)\">\\(\\mathcal E\\subset D(B)\\)<\/span> be a dense linear subspace on which <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(\\|B\\xi\\|=\\|\\xi\\|\\)\">\\(\\|B\\xi\\|=\\|\\xi\\|\\)<\/span>. Then <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(B=1\\)\">\\(B=1\\)<\/span>. Indeed, for any <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(\\xi\\in H\\)\">\\(\\xi\\in H\\)<\/span>, choose <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(\\xi_n\\in\\mathcal E\\)\">\\(\\xi_n\\in\\mathcal E\\)<\/span> tending to <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(\\xi\\)\">\\(\\xi\\)<\/span>. Linearity gives\n<span class=\"math display\" data-tex=\"\\[\n \\|B\\xi_n-B\\xi_m\\|=\\|\\xi_n-\\xi_m\\|,\n\\]\">\\[\n \\|B\\xi_n-B\\xi_m\\|=\\|\\xi_n-\\xi_m\\|,\n\\]<\/span>\nso <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(B\\xi_n\\)\">\\(B\\xi_n\\)<\/span> converges. Closedness puts <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(\\xi\\)\">\\(\\xi\\)<\/span> in <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(D(B)\\)\">\\(D(B)\\)<\/span> and identifies that limit with <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(B\\xi\\)\">\\(B\\xi\\)<\/span>. Thus <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(D(B)=H\\)\">\\(D(B)=H\\)<\/span> and <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(B\\)\">\\(B\\)<\/span> is an isometry. Polarization gives <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(B^*B=1\\)\">\\(B^*B=1\\)<\/span>; since <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(B=B^*\\geq0\\)\">\\(B=B^*\\geq0\\)<\/span>, uniqueness of the positive square root of <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(1\\)\">\\(1\\)<\/span>, proved in <a href=\"../courses/OA-FLOW/OA-FLOW-CF.html#oa-flow.cf.9\">CF9<\/a>, gives <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(B=1\\)\">\\(B=1\\)<\/span>. The approximating sequence is chosen separately for each vector; the Hilbert space need not be separable.<\/p>\n<p><strong>GNS uniqueness criterion.<\/strong> Let <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(\\Phi,\\Psi\\)\">\\(\\Phi,\\Psi\\)<\/span> be faithful normal semifinite weights on a von Neumann algebra <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(N\\)\">\\(N\\)<\/span>, with the same modular automorphism group. Suppose a linear subspace <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(\\mathcal C\\subset\\mathfrak n_\\Phi\\)\">\\(\\mathcal C\\subset\\mathfrak n_\\Phi\\)<\/span> satisfies\n<span class=\"math display\" data-tex=\"\\[\n \\overline{\\Lambda_\\Phi(\\mathcal C)}=H_\\Phi,\\qquad\n \\Psi(a^*a)=\\Phi(a^*a)\\quad(a\\in\\mathcal C).\n\\]\">\\[\n \\overline{\\Lambda_\\Phi(\\mathcal C)}=H_\\Phi,\\qquad\n \\Psi(a^*a)=\\Phi(a^*a)\\quad(a\\in\\mathcal C).\n\\]<\/span>\nThen <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(\\Psi=\\Phi\\)\">\\(\\Psi=\\Phi\\)<\/span> on the entire positive cone. No algebra or multiplication-invariance condition on <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(\\mathcal C\\)\">\\(\\mathcal C\\)<\/span> is required.<\/p>\n<p><strong>Central density.<\/strong> We first derive the density needed for the criterion from the earlier local proofs. If faithful n.s.f. weights <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(\\rho,\\nu\\)\">\\(\\rho,\\nu\\)<\/span> have <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\((D\\rho:D\\nu)_r=1\\)\">\\((D\\rho:D\\nu)_r=1\\)<\/span>, <a href=\"../courses/OA-FLOW/OA-FLOW-BC.html#bc-4\">BC4<\/a> gives the constant off-diagonal orbit <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(\\beta_r^{\\rho,\\nu}(1)=1\\)\">\\(\\beta_r^{\\rho,\\nu}(1)=1\\)<\/span>. Its norm-entire extension has values one at <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(-i\\)\">\\(-i\\)<\/span> and <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(-i/2\\)\">\\(-i/2\\)<\/span>. <a href=\"../courses/OA-FLOW/OA-FLOW-MA.html#oa-flow.ma.4\">MA4<\/a>, equation MA11 with <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(k=1\\)\">\\(k=1\\)<\/span>, gives both inequalities <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(\\rho(h)\\leq\\nu(h)\\)\">\\(\\rho(h)\\leq\\nu(h)\\)<\/span> and <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(\\nu(h)\\leq\\rho(h)\\)\">\\(\\nu(h)\\leq\\rho(h)\\)<\/span>, for every <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(h\\geq0\\)\">\\(h\\geq0\\)<\/span>, including infinite values. The BC chain rule also proves injectivity for cocycles with a fixed reference weight.<\/p>\n<p>Equality of the modular groups makes <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(u_r=(D\\Psi:D\\Phi)_r\\)\">\\(u_r=(D\\Psi:D\\Phi)_r\\)<\/span> central, by BC4. The group fixes the center: a central self-adjoint multiplier commutes with the full finite-star involution and its adjoint, hence with their positive product and imaginary powers, by <a href=\"../courses/OA-FLOW/OA-FLOW-NC.html#oa-flow.nc.4\">NC4<\/a>. The cocycle law is consequently <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(u_{r+s}=u_ru_s\\)\">\\(u_{r+s}=u_ru_s\\)<\/span>. <a href=\"../courses/OA-FLOW/OA-FLOW-RF.html#rf-5\">RF5<\/a> gives the self-adjoint generator <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(A\\)\">\\(A\\)<\/span>, with <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(u_r=e^{irA}\\)\">\\(u_r=e^{irA}\\)<\/span>. Its resolvents and spectral projections commute with every operator commuting with the center, so <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(A\\)\">\\(A\\)<\/span> is affiliated with the center. The full Borel calculus of <a href=\"../courses/OA-FLOW/OA-FLOW-SF.html#oa-flow.sf.sb3\">SF<\/a> gives the positive nonsingular central operator <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(h=e^A\\)\">\\(h=e^A\\)<\/span>. The density construction <a href=\"../courses/OA-FLOW/OA-FLOW-CZ.html#cz-1\">CZ1–6<\/a> supplies a faithful n.s.f. weight <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(\\Phi_h\\)\">\\(\\Phi_h\\)<\/span> with cocycle <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(h^{ir}=u_r\\)\">\\(h^{ir}=u_r\\)<\/span>. Fixed-reference injectivity therefore gives\n&lt;a id=&quot;equation-gda31&quot;&gt;&lt;/a&gt;\n<span class=\"math display\" data-tex=\"\\[\n \\Psi(X)=\\Phi_h(X)\n =\\sup_n\\Phi(h_n^{1/2}Xh_n^{1/2}),\\qquad\n h_n=\\min(h,n),\\quad X\\in N_+.\n \\tag{GDA31}\n\\]\">\\[\n \\Psi(X)=\\Phi_h(X)\n =\\sup_n\\Phi(h_n^{1/2}Xh_n^{1/2}),\\qquad\n h_n=\\min(h,n),\\quad X\\in N_+.\n \\tag{GDA31}\n\\]<\/span>\nTo compare these cutoffs with CZ's resolvent regularizations, use\n<span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(h(1+\\varepsilon h)^{-1}\\leq h_n\\)\">\\(h(1+\\varepsilon h)^{-1}\\leq h_n\\)<\/span> when <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(n\\geq\\varepsilon^{-1}\\)\">\\(n\\geq\\varepsilon^{-1}\\)<\/span>, and\n<span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(h_n\\leq(1+\\varepsilon n)h(1+\\varepsilon h)^{-1}\\)\">\\(h_n\\leq(1+\\varepsilon n)h(1+\\varepsilon h)^{-1}\\)<\/span>.\nCentrality makes these positive-operator inequalities valid inside the weight evaluations. Letting <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(n\\)\">\\(n\\)<\/span> and <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(\\varepsilon^{-1}\\)\">\\(\\varepsilon^{-1}\\)<\/span> increase proves equality of the two suprema, including their infinite values.<\/p>\n<p><strong>Proof of the criterion.<\/strong> Represent <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(N\\)\">\\(N\\)<\/span> faithfully on <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(H_\\Phi\\)\">\\(H_\\Phi\\)<\/span>, as in <a href=\"../courses/OA-FLOW/OA-FLOW-NF.html#oa-flow.nf.5\">NF5<\/a>, and transport the spectral projections of <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(h\\)\">\\(h\\)<\/span> by this normal representation. Let <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(B=\\pi_\\Phi(h)^{1/2}\\)\">\\(B=\\pi_\\Phi(h)^{1/2}\\)<\/span> be the resulting closed positive operator, with its full domain from <a href=\"../courses/OA-FLOW/OA-FLOW-SF.html#oa-flow.sf.sb3\">SB3–4<\/a>. For <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(a\\in\\mathfrak n_\\Phi\\)\">\\(a\\in\\mathfrak n_\\Phi\\)<\/span>, centrality and the bounded GNS identity give\n&lt;a id=&quot;equation-gda32&quot;&gt;&lt;/a&gt;\n<span class=\"math display\" data-tex=\"\\[\n \\begin{aligned}\n \\Psi(a^*a)\n &amp;=\\sup_n\\Phi(a^*h_n a)\\\\\n &amp;=\\sup_n\\|\\pi_\\Phi(h_n)^{1/2}\\Lambda_\\Phi(a)\\|^2\n =\\|B\\Lambda_\\Phi(a)\\|^2 .\n \\end{aligned}\n \\tag{GDA32}\n\\]\">\\[\n \\begin{aligned}\n \\Psi(a^*a)\n &amp;=\\sup_n\\Phi(a^*h_n a)\\\\\n &amp;=\\sup_n\\|\\pi_\\Phi(h_n)^{1/2}\\Lambda_\\Phi(a)\\|^2\n =\\|B\\Lambda_\\Phi(a)\\|^2 .\n \\end{aligned}\n \\tag{GDA32}\n\\]<\/span>\nThe last expression is interpreted as infinity off <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(D(B)\\)\">\\(D(B)\\)<\/span>; its equality with the supremum is precisely the positive spectral-domain criterion. On <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(\\mathcal E=\\Lambda_\\Phi(\\mathcal C)\\)\">\\(\\mathcal E=\\Lambda_\\Phi(\\mathcal C)\\)<\/span> the hypothesis makes it finite and gives <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(\\|B\\xi\\|=\\|\\xi\\|\\)\">\\(\\|B\\xi\\|=\\|\\xi\\|\\)<\/span>. The dense-space observation proves <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(B=1\\)\">\\(B=1\\)<\/span>. Faithfulness of <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(\\pi_\\Phi\\)\">\\(\\pi_\\Phi\\)<\/span>, applied to the spectral projections, then gives <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(h=1\\)\">\\(h=1\\)<\/span>. Equation (GDA31) proves <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(\\Psi=\\Phi\\)\">\\(\\Psi=\\Phi\\)<\/span> on all <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(N_+\\)\">\\(N_+\\)<\/span>. Only the closed operator is extended from a dense set; no continuity of an unbounded weight on a dense positive subset is assumed. <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(\\square\\)\">\\(\\square\\)<\/span><\/p>\n<p><strong>Application to the dual weight.<\/strong> Take <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(\\Phi=\\widehat\\varphi\\)\">\\(\\Phi=\\widehat\\varphi\\)<\/span>, the weight constructed by GDW. Suppose a faithful n.s.f. weight <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(\\Psi\\)\">\\(\\Psi\\)<\/span> has its two modular generator formulas and the same quadratic values at every <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(L_x\\)\">\\(L_x\\)<\/span>, <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(x\\in\\mathscr B_\\varphi\\)\">\\(x\\in\\mathscr B_\\varphi\\)<\/span>. Normality and the two generating families give equality of the modular groups on <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(R\\)\">\\(R\\)<\/span>. Use the linear space\n<span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(\\mathcal C=L(\\mathscr D_\\varphi)\\subset L(\\mathscr B_\\varphi)\\)\">\\(\\mathcal C=L(\\mathscr D_\\varphi)\\subset L(\\mathscr B_\\varphi)\\)<\/span>.\n<a href=\"../courses/OA-FLOW/OA-FLOW-GDW.html#gdw-6\">GDW6<\/a> identifies\n<span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(\\Lambda_\\Phi(L_x)=\\eta_\\varphi(x)\\)\">\\(\\Lambda_\\Phi(L_x)=\\eta_\\varphi(x)\\)<\/span>, and <a href=\"../courses/OA-FLOW/OA-FLOW-GDW.html#gdw-2\">GDW2<\/a> proves that <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(\\eta_\\varphi(\\mathscr D_\\varphi)\\)\">\\(\\eta_\\varphi(\\mathscr D_\\varphi)\\)<\/span> is dense in the whole <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(L^2(G,H_\\varphi)\\)\">\\(L^2(G,H_\\varphi)\\)<\/span>. The GNS criterion therefore applies and yields\n&lt;a id=&quot;equation-gda33&quot;&gt;&lt;/a&gt;\n<span class=\"math display\" data-tex=\"\\[\n \\Psi=\\widehat\\varphi\\quad\\hbox{on the entire positive cone}.\n \\tag{GDA33}\n\\]\">\\[\n \\Psi=\\widehat\\varphi\\quad\\hbox{on the entire positive cone}.\n \\tag{GDA33}\n\\]<\/span>\nThe group, algebra and Hilbert space retain their full arbitrary scope. The classical dual-weight characterization appears in Takesaki, <a href=\"https://doi.org/10.1007/978-3-662-10451-4\"><em>Theory of Operator Algebras II<\/em>, Chapter X, Lemma 1.18, printed p.250<\/a>; the criterion above uses the closed positive density operator on the GNS Hilbert space.<\/p>\n<h3>The closed-graph mechanism<\/h3>\n<p><a class=\"figure-link\" href=\"../courses/OA-FLOW/assets/gns-weight-uniqueness/gns-weight-uniqueness.png\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\"><img src=\"../courses/OA-FLOW/assets/gns-weight-uniqueness/gns-weight-uniqueness.png\" alt=\"Central density, the GNS quadratic identity, closed-graph extension and positivity\" /><span>Open figure at full size<\/span><\/a><\/p>\n<p>The four boxes display the actual proof maps and domains. The density <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(h\\)\">\\(h\\)<\/span> is central and nonsingular; <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(B=\\pi_\\Phi(h)^{1/2}\\)\">\\(B=\\pi_\\Phi(h)^{1/2}\\)<\/span> acts on <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(H_\\Phi\\)\">\\(H_\\Phi\\)<\/span>, and <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(\\mathcal E=\\Lambda_\\Phi(\\mathcal C)\\)\">\\(\\mathcal E=\\Lambda_\\Phi(\\mathcal C)\\)<\/span> is a linear dense subspace of its domain. Equality (GDA32) makes <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(B|_{\\mathcal E}\\)\">\\(B|_{\\mathcal E}\\)<\/span> isometric. For every <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(\\xi\\in H_\\Phi\\)\">\\(\\xi\\in H_\\Phi\\)<\/span>, the two coordinates of <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\((\\xi_n,B\\xi_n)\\)\">\\((\\xi_n,B\\xi_n)\\)<\/span> converge, so the closed graph puts their limit in the graph of <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(B\\)\">\\(B\\)<\/span>. This proves the full domain assertion before positivity forces <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(B=1\\)\">\\(B=1\\)<\/span>. A general isometry need not be the identity; positivity is the essential final hypothesis. The diagram is a logical schematic of this arbitrary-dimensional argument, not a numerical or finite-dimensional approximation.<\/p>\n<p>The local proofs used for the central density are MA4, BC4, NC4, RF5 and CZ1–6, as specified above. <a href=\"../courses/OA-FLOW/assets/gns-weight-uniqueness/gns-weight-uniqueness.svg\">Editable SVG<\/a>, <a href=\"../courses/OA-FLOW/assets/gns-weight-uniqueness/data.json\">exact diagram labels<\/a>, and <a href=\"../courses/OA-FLOW/assets/gns-weight-uniqueness/render.py\">reproduction source<\/a> accompany the figure. The diagram and caption are CC0-1.0 to the extent of rights held; DejaVu font terms remain applicable.<\/p>\n","source":"docs/courses/OA-FLOW/src/OA-FLOW-GDA.md","start_offset":25458,"end_offset":32435,"role":"registered_statement_and_proof_passage"},"9edfce3cd47a7d2c9327b79840a121fbee0ea18d1bcd2f4b3acf76e5454c1049":{"markdown":"<a id=\"gda-11\"><\/a>\n\n## GDA11. The balanced derivative under the action\n\nLet \\(\\varphi,\\psi\\) be faithful normal semifinite weights on \\(M\\), set \\(\\chi=\\varphi\\oplus\\psi\\) on \\(M_2(M)\\), and let \\(\\beta=\\alpha\\otimes\\mathrm{id}_2\\). For fixed \\(s\\in G\\), write \\(\\varphi_s=\\varphi\\circ\\alpha_s\\) and \\(\\psi_s=\\psi\\circ\\alpha_s\\). [BC1](OA-FLOW-BC.md#bc-1) gives \\(\\chi\\circ\\beta_s=\\varphi_s\\oplus\\psi_s\\) on the whole positive cone. In particular its second diagonal summand is \\(\\psi_s\\).\n\n**Theorem.** For every \\(s\\in G\\) and \\(r\\in\\mathbb R\\),\n\n<a id=\"equation-gdab1\"><\/a>\n\n\\[\n (D(\\chi\\circ\\beta_s):D\\chi)_r\n =\\begin{pmatrix}\n  (D(\\varphi\\circ\\alpha_s):D\\varphi)_r&0\\\\\n  0&(D(\\psi\\circ\\alpha_s):D\\psi)_r\n \\end{pmatrix}.\n \\tag{GDAB1}\n\\]\n\n**Proof.** Identify \\(M_2(M_2(M))\\) with \\(M_4(M)\\), ordering the diagonal entries as \\((\\varphi,\\psi,\\varphi_s,\\psi_s)\\). BC1 identifies the outer balanced weight \\(\\Theta(\\chi,\\chi\\circ\\beta_s)\\) with this four-weight matrix weight, including all finite GNS domains. The outer lower-left matrix unit becomes \\(E_{31}+E_{42}\\). [BC3's two-entry corner independence](OA-FLOW-BC.md#bc-3) and [BC4's normalized derivative definition](OA-FLOW-BC.md#bc-4) give\n\n<a id=\"equation-gdab2\"><\/a>\n\n\\[\n \\sigma_r^{\\Theta_4}(E_{31})=(D\\varphi_s:D\\varphi)_rE_{31},\n \\qquad\n \\sigma_r^{\\Theta_4}(E_{42})=(D\\psi_s:D\\psi)_rE_{42}.\n \\tag{GDAB2}\n\\]\n\nApply the modular group to their sum and read its outer lower-left block. BC4 gives precisely(GDAB1). This determines the normalized unitary itself, without an undetermined central phase. Only the full finite-matrix corner construction is used; compression by an arbitrary centralizer projection is unnecessary. \\(\\square\\)","html":"<h2>GDA11. The balanced derivative under the action<\/h2>\n<p>Let <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(\\varphi,\\psi\\)\">\\(\\varphi,\\psi\\)<\/span> be faithful normal semifinite weights on <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(M\\)\">\\(M\\)<\/span>, set <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(\\chi=\\varphi\\oplus\\psi\\)\">\\(\\chi=\\varphi\\oplus\\psi\\)<\/span> on <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(M_2(M)\\)\">\\(M_2(M)\\)<\/span>, and let <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(\\beta=\\alpha\\otimes\\mathrm{id}_2\\)\">\\(\\beta=\\alpha\\otimes\\mathrm{id}_2\\)<\/span>. For fixed <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(s\\in G\\)\">\\(s\\in G\\)<\/span>, write <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(\\varphi_s=\\varphi\\circ\\alpha_s\\)\">\\(\\varphi_s=\\varphi\\circ\\alpha_s\\)<\/span> and <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(\\psi_s=\\psi\\circ\\alpha_s\\)\">\\(\\psi_s=\\psi\\circ\\alpha_s\\)<\/span>. <a href=\"../courses/OA-FLOW/OA-FLOW-BC.html#bc-1\">BC1<\/a> gives <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(\\chi\\circ\\beta_s=\\varphi_s\\oplus\\psi_s\\)\">\\(\\chi\\circ\\beta_s=\\varphi_s\\oplus\\psi_s\\)<\/span> on the whole positive cone. In particular its second diagonal summand is <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(\\psi_s\\)\">\\(\\psi_s\\)<\/span>.<\/p>\n<p><strong>Theorem.<\/strong> For every <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(s\\in G\\)\">\\(s\\in G\\)<\/span> and <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(r\\in\\mathbb R\\)\">\\(r\\in\\mathbb R\\)<\/span>,<\/p>\n<p><span class=\"math display\" data-tex=\"\\[\n (D(\\chi\\circ\\beta_s):D\\chi)_r\n =\\begin{pmatrix}\n  (D(\\varphi\\circ\\alpha_s):D\\varphi)_r&amp;0\\\\\n  0&amp;(D(\\psi\\circ\\alpha_s):D\\psi)_r\n \\end{pmatrix}.\n \\tag{GDAB1}\n\\]\">\\[\n (D(\\chi\\circ\\beta_s):D\\chi)_r\n =\\begin{pmatrix}\n  (D(\\varphi\\circ\\alpha_s):D\\varphi)_r&amp;0\\\\\n  0&amp;(D(\\psi\\circ\\alpha_s):D\\psi)_r\n \\end{pmatrix}.\n \\tag{GDAB1}\n\\]<\/span><\/p>\n<p><strong>Proof.<\/strong> Identify <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(M_2(M_2(M))\\)\">\\(M_2(M_2(M))\\)<\/span> with <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(M_4(M)\\)\">\\(M_4(M)\\)<\/span>, ordering the diagonal entries as <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\((\\varphi,\\psi,\\varphi_s,\\psi_s)\\)\">\\((\\varphi,\\psi,\\varphi_s,\\psi_s)\\)<\/span>. BC1 identifies the outer balanced weight <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(\\Theta(\\chi,\\chi\\circ\\beta_s)\\)\">\\(\\Theta(\\chi,\\chi\\circ\\beta_s)\\)<\/span> with this four-weight matrix weight, including all finite GNS domains. The outer lower-left matrix unit becomes <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(E_{31}+E_{42}\\)\">\\(E_{31}+E_{42}\\)<\/span>. <a href=\"../courses/OA-FLOW/OA-FLOW-BC.html#bc-3\">BC3's two-entry corner independence<\/a> and <a href=\"../courses/OA-FLOW/OA-FLOW-BC.html#bc-4\">BC4's normalized derivative definition<\/a> give<\/p>\n<p><span class=\"math display\" data-tex=\"\\[\n \\sigma_r^{\\Theta_4}(E_{31})=(D\\varphi_s:D\\varphi)_rE_{31},\n \\qquad\n \\sigma_r^{\\Theta_4}(E_{42})=(D\\psi_s:D\\psi)_rE_{42}.\n \\tag{GDAB2}\n\\]\">\\[\n \\sigma_r^{\\Theta_4}(E_{31})=(D\\varphi_s:D\\varphi)_rE_{31},\n \\qquad\n \\sigma_r^{\\Theta_4}(E_{42})=(D\\psi_s:D\\psi)_rE_{42}.\n \\tag{GDAB2}\n\\]<\/span><\/p>\n<p>Apply the modular group to their sum and read its outer lower-left block. BC4 gives precisely(GDAB1). This determines the normalized unitary itself, without an undetermined central phase. Only the full finite-matrix corner construction is used; compression by an arbitrary centralizer projection is unnecessary. <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(\\square\\)\">\\(\\square\\)<\/span><\/p>\n","source":"docs/courses/OA-FLOW/src/OA-FLOW-GDA.md","start_offset":44785,"end_offset":46479,"role":"registered_statement_and_proof_passage"},"9ed357fc2088b8ed878dfdee14cffc80078c0d2af4abcf8596d39ea802c1b958":{"markdown":"## NWR6. Compact squares give injectivity for every normal input\n\nChoose \\(h\\in C_c(G)\\) with \\(\\int|h(s)|^2ds=1\\). Such a function exists: the proved compact-cutoff construction gives a nonzero compactly supported continuous function, Haar positivity gives positive squared integral, and compact finiteness gives finiteness; divide by its positive square root. For \\(a\\in M\\), the function \\(x_a(s)=h(s)a\\) is bounded, compactly supported and strongly* continuous, so it is in GDA's compact coefficient algebra \\(\\mathscr K\\). Let \\(L_{x_a}\\) be its bounded normal integrated operator. GDA6's whole bounded-square formula, independent of any finite-weight hypothesis, says\n\n<a id=\"equation-nwr19\"><\/a>\n\n\\[\n T(L_{x_a}^*L_{x_a})\n =\\pi\\left(\\int |h(s)|^2a^*a\\,ds\\right)=\\pi(a^*a).\n\\tag{NWR19}\n\\]\nConsequently, for every normal weight \\(\\varphi\\),\n\n<a id=\"equation-nwr20\"><\/a>\n\n\\[\n D\\varphi(L_{x_a}^*L_{x_a})=\\varphi(a^*a),\n\\tag{NWR20}\n\\]\nincluding an infinite value. Equal dual weights therefore have equal values at every \\(a^*a\\). CF7's bounded positive square root writes every \\(b\\in M_+\\) as \\((b^{1/2})^*b^{1/2}\\); thus they agree on the entire positive cone. This proves injectivity without extending unitary cocycles to nonfaithful weights. EP6/GDA8 show that every normal input has a normal dual, and DA proves its invariance on the entire positive cone. Together with NWR3–5, these facts prove NWR0; NWR3–4 and injectivity give the stated restriction to semifinite weights.","html":"<h2>NWR6. Compact squares give injectivity for every normal input<\/h2>\n<p>Choose <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(h\\in C_c(G)\\)\">\\(h\\in C_c(G)\\)<\/span> with <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(\\int|h(s)|^2ds=1\\)\">\\(\\int|h(s)|^2ds=1\\)<\/span>. Such a function exists: the proved compact-cutoff construction gives a nonzero compactly supported continuous function, Haar positivity gives positive squared integral, and compact finiteness gives finiteness; divide by its positive square root. For <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(a\\in M\\)\">\\(a\\in M\\)<\/span>, the function <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(x_a(s)=h(s)a\\)\">\\(x_a(s)=h(s)a\\)<\/span> is bounded, compactly supported and strongly* continuous, so it is in GDA's compact coefficient algebra <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(\\mathscr K\\)\">\\(\\mathscr K\\)<\/span>. Let <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(L_{x_a}\\)\">\\(L_{x_a}\\)<\/span> be its bounded normal integrated operator. GDA6's whole bounded-square formula, independent of any finite-weight hypothesis, says<\/p>\n<p><span class=\"math display\" data-tex=\"\\[\n T(L_{x_a}^*L_{x_a})\n =\\pi\\left(\\int |h(s)|^2a^*a\\,ds\\right)=\\pi(a^*a).\n\\tag{NWR19}\n\\]\">\\[\n T(L_{x_a}^*L_{x_a})\n =\\pi\\left(\\int |h(s)|^2a^*a\\,ds\\right)=\\pi(a^*a).\n\\tag{NWR19}\n\\]<\/span>\nConsequently, for every normal weight <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(\\varphi\\)\">\\(\\varphi\\)<\/span>,<\/p>\n<p><span class=\"math display\" data-tex=\"\\[\n D\\varphi(L_{x_a}^*L_{x_a})=\\varphi(a^*a),\n\\tag{NWR20}\n\\]\">\\[\n D\\varphi(L_{x_a}^*L_{x_a})=\\varphi(a^*a),\n\\tag{NWR20}\n\\]<\/span>\nincluding an infinite value. Equal dual weights therefore have equal values at every <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(a^*a\\)\">\\(a^*a\\)<\/span>. CF7's bounded positive square root writes every <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(b\\in M_+\\)\">\\(b\\in M_+\\)<\/span> as <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\((b^{1/2})^*b^{1/2}\\)\">\\((b^{1/2})^*b^{1/2}\\)<\/span>; thus they agree on the entire positive cone. This proves injectivity without extending unitary cocycles to nonfaithful weights. EP6/GDA8 show that every normal input has a normal dual, and DA proves its invariance on the entire positive cone. Together with NWR3–5, these facts prove NWR0; NWR3–4 and injectivity give the stated restriction to semifinite weights.<\/p>\n","source":"docs/courses/OA-FLOW/src/OA-FLOW-NWR.md","start_offset":14672,"end_offset":16153,"role":"registered_statement_and_proof_passage"},"9ec180035a550a4c9c75b679b3d76000ef8a161ae55b697235d53addbad6b1ee":{"markdown":"<a id=\"l129-amplified-freeness\"><\/a>\n## Freeness survives the matrix cocycle perturbation\n\nFix $s\\ne e$, and suppose $C\\in A$ satisfies\n\n<a id=\"equation-t9\"><\/a>\n\n$$\nCX=\\gamma_s(X)C\n\\qquad(X\\in A).\n\\tag{T9}\n$$\n\nSet $D=d_s^*C$.  Then\n\n<a id=\"equation-t10\"><\/a>\n\n$$\nDX=(\\alpha_s\\otimes\\operatorname{id})(X)D.\n\\tag{T10}\n$$\n\nTaking $X=1\\otimes b$, $b\\in M_2(\\mathbb C)$, shows that $D$ commutes\nwith the full scalar matrix algebra.  Hence $D=c\\otimes1_2$ for some\n$c\\in M$.  Taking $X=x\\otimes e_{11}$ in (T10) gives\n\n<a id=\"equation-t11\"><\/a>\n\n$$\ncx=\\alpha_s(x)c\n\\qquad(x\\in M).\n\\tag{T11}\n$$\n\nFreeness of $\\alpha_s$ makes $c=0$, and therefore $C=0$.  Thus\n\n<a id=\"equation-t12\"><\/a>\n\n$$\n\\boxed{\\gamma\\text{ is free}.}\n\\tag{T12}\n$$\n\nThis calculation proves both stability operations actually used here:\nmatrix amplification and perturbation by the specified cocycle do not destroy\nfreeness.","html":"<h2>Freeness survives the matrix cocycle perturbation<\/h2>\n<p>Fix <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(s\\ne e\\)\">\\(s\\ne e\\)<\/span>, and suppose <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(C\\in A\\)\">\\(C\\in A\\)<\/span> satisfies<\/p>\n<p><span class=\"math display\" data-tex=\"\\[\nCX=\\gamma_s(X)C\n\\qquad(X\\in A).\n\\tag{T9}\n\\]\">\\[\nCX=\\gamma_s(X)C\n\\qquad(X\\in A).\n\\tag{T9}\n\\]<\/span><\/p>\n<p>Set <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(D=d_s^*C\\)\">\\(D=d_s^*C\\)<\/span>.  Then<\/p>\n<p><span class=\"math display\" data-tex=\"\\[\nDX=(\\alpha_s\\otimes\\operatorname{id})(X)D.\n\\tag{T10}\n\\]\">\\[\nDX=(\\alpha_s\\otimes\\operatorname{id})(X)D.\n\\tag{T10}\n\\]<\/span><\/p>\n<p>Taking <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(X=1\\otimes b\\)\">\\(X=1\\otimes b\\)<\/span>, <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(b\\in M_2(\\mathbb C)\\)\">\\(b\\in M_2(\\mathbb C)\\)<\/span>, shows that <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(D\\)\">\\(D\\)<\/span> commutes\nwith the full scalar matrix algebra.  Hence <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(D=c\\otimes1_2\\)\">\\(D=c\\otimes1_2\\)<\/span> for some\n<span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(c\\in M\\)\">\\(c\\in M\\)<\/span>.  Taking <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(X=x\\otimes e_{11}\\)\">\\(X=x\\otimes e_{11}\\)<\/span> in (T10) gives<\/p>\n<p><span class=\"math display\" data-tex=\"\\[\ncx=\\alpha_s(x)c\n\\qquad(x\\in M).\n\\tag{T11}\n\\]\">\\[\ncx=\\alpha_s(x)c\n\\qquad(x\\in M).\n\\tag{T11}\n\\]<\/span><\/p>\n<p>Freeness of <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(\\alpha_s\\)\">\\(\\alpha_s\\)<\/span> makes <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(c=0\\)\">\\(c=0\\)<\/span>, and therefore <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(C=0\\)\">\\(C=0\\)<\/span>.  Thus<\/p>\n<p><span class=\"math display\" data-tex=\"\\[\n\\boxed{\\gamma\\text{ is free}.}\n\\tag{T12}\n\\]\">\\[\n\\boxed{\\gamma\\text{ is free}.}\n\\tag{T12}\n\\]<\/span><\/p>\n<p>This calculation proves both stability operations actually used here:\nmatrix amplification and perturbation by the specified cocycle do not destroy\nfreeness.<\/p>\n","source":"docs/courses/OA-FLOW/src/OA-FLOW-L129.md","start_offset":27592,"end_offset":28478,"role":"registered_statement_and_proof_passage"},"9e5478e35ba30250a8f7c0610061093f6886ec48249b1a459498a4661af78d7f":{"markdown":"<a id=\"oa-flow.field.matrices\"><\/a>\n\n## Scalar diagonals and measurable matrices on one Hilbert space\n\nSet\n\\[\n \\mathcal H=L^2(S,\\nu)\\otimes H_0\n             =L^2(S,\\nu;H_0),\\qquad\n \\mathcal D=L^\\infty(S,\\nu)\\bar\\otimes B(H_0).\n \\tag{A9}\n\\]\nThe Hilbert identification sends \\(f\\otimes\\xi\\) to \\(y\\mapsto f(y)\\xi\\). In the basis \\((e_j)\\) its squared norm is \\(\\sum_j\\|f_j\\|_2^2\\), so finite-coordinate fields are dense and completion proves that it is onto. A measurable \\(H_0\\)-valued field here means one with measurable scalar coordinates; its finite-coordinate truncations show strong measurability and supply a Borel representative after changing a null set. This includes the finite-basis case.\n\n**Matrix-field lemma.** The following are exactly the same operators:\n\n1. Operators on \\(\\mathcal H\\) commuting with every \\(M_f\\otimes1\\), \\(f\\in L^\\infty(S,\\nu)\\).\n2. Elements of \\(\\mathcal D\\).\n3. Fields \\(y\\mapsto T_y\\in B(H_0)\\) with Borel scalar matrix entries, bounded in operator norm outside a \\(\\nu\\)-null set, acting by \\((T\\xi)(y)=T_y\\xi(y)\\).\n\nFields are identified if they agree outside one null set. Their algebra operations and positivity are pointwise, and\n\\[\n \\|T\\|=\\operatorname*{ess\\,sup}_{y\\in S}\\|T_y\\|,\n \\qquad\n (L^\\infty(S,\\nu)\\otimes1)'=\\mathcal D.\n \\tag{A10}\n\\]\n\n**Proof.** Let \\(V_jf=f\\otimes e_j\\). If \\(T\\) commutes with the scalar diagonal, each \\(T_{ij}=V_i^*TV_j\\) commutes with all scalar multiplication on \\(L^2(S,\\nu)\\). The [scalar multiplier theorem IW1](OA-FLOW-IW.md#iw-1) says \\(T_{ij}=M_{t_{ij}}\\), with \\(t_{ij}\\in L^\\infty(S,\\nu)\\). Choose Borel representatives for this finite or countable family.\n\nFor finite-coordinate vectors \\(u,v\\) with rational complex coordinates, compression of \\(T\\) between the maps \\(f\\mapsto f\\otimes u\\) and \\(f\\mapsto f\\otimes v\\) is multiplication by\n\\(\\sum_{i,j}t_{ij}(y)u_j\\overline{v_i}\\), with norm at most \\(\\|T\\|\\|u\\|\\|v\\|\\). Thus\n\\[\n \\left|\\sum_{i,j}t_{ij}(y)u_j\\overline{v_i}\\right|\n \\le\\|T\\|\\|u\\|\\|v\\|\n \\tag{A11}\n\\]\noutside a null set. There are only countably many such pairs; remove their union of exceptional Borel null sets. Density of rational-coordinate vectors extends (A11) to a bounded sesquilinear form on all of \\(H_0\\), for every remaining \\(y\\). The [Hilbert representation theorem](OA-FLOW-CF.md#oa-flow.cf.8) gives a unique \\(T_y\\in B(H_0)\\) of norm at most \\(\\|T\\|\\) with the prescribed entries. Set \\(T_y=0\\) on the discarded set.\n\nConversely, given such a field with bound \\(C\\), every coordinate of \\(T_y\\xi(y)\\) is the pointwise limit of its finite-coordinate sums and is measurable. The pointwise bound gives\n\\(\\int\\|T_y\\xi(y)\\|^2d\\nu\\le C^2\\int\\|\\xi(y)\\|^2d\\nu\\), so the rule defines a bounded operator commuting with the scalar diagonal. Its entries are the original \\(M_{t_{ij}}\\). In particular this construction recovers the preceding \\(T\\), since matrix entries determine a bounded operator. It proves equality of the operator norm with the essential supremum: one inequality is the integral bound, and the other follows by applying (A11) to the resulting operator. The function \\(y\\mapsto\\|T_y\\|\\) is measurable, as the supremum of countably many normalized rational-vector tests.\n\nPointwise adjoints have conjugate-transposed entries. Products act pointwise by composition on every vector field; their entries are the convergent sums \\(\\sum_k t_{ik}(y)s_{kj}(y)\\), whose absolute convergence follows from Cauchy–Schwarz for the two \\(\\ell^2\\) rows and columns. Positivity is also pointwise: countably many rational quadratic-form tests give one common set on which \\(T_y\\ge0\\) when \\(T\\ge0\\), and the converse follows by integration. Equality of two operators is equality of their countably many entries outside one common null set, hence equality of their fields there.\n\nFinally let \\(P_n=1\\otimes p_n\\), where \\(p_n\\) projects onto the first \\(n\\) basis vectors, and take \\(p_n=1\\) once a finite basis is exhausted. Then\n\\[\n P_nTP_n=\\sum_{i,j\\le n}M_{t_{ij}}\\otimes|e_i\\rangle\\langle e_j|\n       \\longrightarrow T\\quad\\text{strongly, together with adjoints}.\n \\tag{A12}\n\\]\nThe bounds are \\(\\|P_nTP_n\\|\\le\\|T\\|\\); convergence follows by expanding\n\\(P_nT(P_n-1)\\xi+(P_n-1)T\\xi\\), and likewise for \\(T^*\\). Thus \\(T\\in\\mathcal D\\). The reverse inclusion follows from commutation of the tensor generators with the scalar diagonal. This proves all assertions. \\(\\square\\) The same proof applies to any nonzero separable Hilbert space in place of \\(H_0\\); its only Hilbert-space input was a finite or countable orthonormal basis.\n\nDefine measurable unitary fields\n\\[\n U_n(y)=R_G(h_n(y)).\n \\tag{A13}\n\\]\nTheir entries are Borel, since the selectors are Borel and \\(R_G\\) is strongly continuous; the lemma gives unitaries \\(U_n\\in\\mathcal D\\). Put\n\\[\n \\begin{aligned}\n \\mathcal D_H\n &=\\mathcal D\\cap\\{U_n:n\\ge1\\}'\\\\\n &=\\{T\\in\\mathcal D:\n       T_yR_G(h)=R_G(h)T_y\\ (h\\in H_y)\n       \\text{ for almost every }y\\}.\n \\end{aligned}\n \\tag{A14}\n\\]\nThe second equality has a precise common-null-set meaning. For \\(T\\) in the first algebra, the countably many equations \\(TU_n=U_nT\\) and the matrix lemma give one conull Borel set on which all corresponding field equations hold. For each \\(y\\) there, density of \\(\\{h_n(y)\\}\\) in \\(H_y\\) and strong continuity extend commutation to every \\(h\\in H_y\\). The converse is immediate. The first line makes \\(\\mathcal D_H\\) a von Neumann algebra, as an intersection of commutants; no interchange of a fiber commutant and a direct integral has been used.","html":"<h2>Scalar diagonals and measurable matrices on one Hilbert space<\/h2>\n<p>Set\n<span class=\"math display\" data-tex=\"\\[\n \\mathcal H=L^2(S,\\nu)\\otimes H_0\n             =L^2(S,\\nu;H_0),\\qquad\n \\mathcal D=L^\\infty(S,\\nu)\\bar\\otimes B(H_0).\n \\tag{A9}\n\\]\">\\[\n \\mathcal H=L^2(S,\\nu)\\otimes H_0\n             =L^2(S,\\nu;H_0),\\qquad\n \\mathcal D=L^\\infty(S,\\nu)\\bar\\otimes B(H_0).\n \\tag{A9}\n\\]<\/span>\nThe Hilbert identification sends <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(f\\otimes\\xi\\)\">\\(f\\otimes\\xi\\)<\/span> to <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(y\\mapsto f(y)\\xi\\)\">\\(y\\mapsto f(y)\\xi\\)<\/span>. In the basis <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\((e_j)\\)\">\\((e_j)\\)<\/span> its squared norm is <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(\\sum_j\\|f_j\\|_2^2\\)\">\\(\\sum_j\\|f_j\\|_2^2\\)<\/span>, so finite-coordinate fields are dense and completion proves that it is onto. A measurable <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(H_0\\)\">\\(H_0\\)<\/span>-valued field here means one with measurable scalar coordinates; its finite-coordinate truncations show strong measurability and supply a Borel representative after changing a null set. This includes the finite-basis case.<\/p>\n<p><strong>Matrix-field lemma.<\/strong> The following are exactly the same operators:<\/p>\n<ol>\n<li>Operators on <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(\\mathcal H\\)\">\\(\\mathcal H\\)<\/span> commuting with every <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(M_f\\otimes1\\)\">\\(M_f\\otimes1\\)<\/span>, <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(f\\in L^\\infty(S,\\nu)\\)\">\\(f\\in L^\\infty(S,\\nu)\\)<\/span>.<\/li>\n<li>Elements of <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(\\mathcal D\\)\">\\(\\mathcal D\\)<\/span>.<\/li>\n<li>Fields <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(y\\mapsto T_y\\in B(H_0)\\)\">\\(y\\mapsto T_y\\in B(H_0)\\)<\/span> with Borel scalar matrix entries, bounded in operator norm outside a <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(\\nu\\)\">\\(\\nu\\)<\/span>-null set, acting by <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\((T\\xi)(y)=T_y\\xi(y)\\)\">\\((T\\xi)(y)=T_y\\xi(y)\\)<\/span>.<\/li>\n<\/ol>\n<p>Fields are identified if they agree outside one null set. Their algebra operations and positivity are pointwise, and\n<span class=\"math display\" data-tex=\"\\[\n \\|T\\|=\\operatorname*{ess\\,sup}_{y\\in S}\\|T_y\\|,\n \\qquad\n (L^\\infty(S,\\nu)\\otimes1)&#x27;=\\mathcal D.\n \\tag{A10}\n\\]\">\\[\n \\|T\\|=\\operatorname*{ess\\,sup}_{y\\in S}\\|T_y\\|,\n \\qquad\n (L^\\infty(S,\\nu)\\otimes1)&#x27;=\\mathcal D.\n \\tag{A10}\n\\]<\/span><\/p>\n<p><strong>Proof.<\/strong> Let <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(V_jf=f\\otimes e_j\\)\">\\(V_jf=f\\otimes e_j\\)<\/span>. If <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(T\\)\">\\(T\\)<\/span> commutes with the scalar diagonal, each <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(T_{ij}=V_i^*TV_j\\)\">\\(T_{ij}=V_i^*TV_j\\)<\/span> commutes with all scalar multiplication on <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(L^2(S,\\nu)\\)\">\\(L^2(S,\\nu)\\)<\/span>. The <a href=\"../courses/OA-FLOW/OA-FLOW-IW.html#iw-1\">scalar multiplier theorem IW1<\/a> says <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(T_{ij}=M_{t_{ij}}\\)\">\\(T_{ij}=M_{t_{ij}}\\)<\/span>, with <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(t_{ij}\\in L^\\infty(S,\\nu)\\)\">\\(t_{ij}\\in L^\\infty(S,\\nu)\\)<\/span>. Choose Borel representatives for this finite or countable family.<\/p>\n<p>For finite-coordinate vectors <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(u,v\\)\">\\(u,v\\)<\/span> with rational complex coordinates, compression of <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(T\\)\">\\(T\\)<\/span> between the maps <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(f\\mapsto f\\otimes u\\)\">\\(f\\mapsto f\\otimes u\\)<\/span> and <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(f\\mapsto f\\otimes v\\)\">\\(f\\mapsto f\\otimes v\\)<\/span> is multiplication by\n<span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(\\sum_{i,j}t_{ij}(y)u_j\\overline{v_i}\\)\">\\(\\sum_{i,j}t_{ij}(y)u_j\\overline{v_i}\\)<\/span>, with norm at most <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(\\|T\\|\\|u\\|\\|v\\|\\)\">\\(\\|T\\|\\|u\\|\\|v\\|\\)<\/span>. Thus\n<span class=\"math display\" data-tex=\"\\[\n \\left|\\sum_{i,j}t_{ij}(y)u_j\\overline{v_i}\\right|\n \\le\\|T\\|\\|u\\|\\|v\\|\n \\tag{A11}\n\\]\">\\[\n \\left|\\sum_{i,j}t_{ij}(y)u_j\\overline{v_i}\\right|\n \\le\\|T\\|\\|u\\|\\|v\\|\n \\tag{A11}\n\\]<\/span>\noutside a null set. There are only countably many such pairs; remove their union of exceptional Borel null sets. Density of rational-coordinate vectors extends (A11) to a bounded sesquilinear form on all of <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(H_0\\)\">\\(H_0\\)<\/span>, for every remaining <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(y\\)\">\\(y\\)<\/span>. The <a href=\"../courses/OA-FLOW/OA-FLOW-CF.html#oa-flow.cf.8\">Hilbert representation theorem<\/a> gives a unique <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(T_y\\in B(H_0)\\)\">\\(T_y\\in B(H_0)\\)<\/span> of norm at most <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(\\|T\\|\\)\">\\(\\|T\\|\\)<\/span> with the prescribed entries. Set <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(T_y=0\\)\">\\(T_y=0\\)<\/span> on the discarded set.<\/p>\n<p>Conversely, given such a field with bound <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(C\\)\">\\(C\\)<\/span>, every coordinate of <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(T_y\\xi(y)\\)\">\\(T_y\\xi(y)\\)<\/span> is the pointwise limit of its finite-coordinate sums and is measurable. The pointwise bound gives\n<span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(\\int\\|T_y\\xi(y)\\|^2d\\nu\\le C^2\\int\\|\\xi(y)\\|^2d\\nu\\)\">\\(\\int\\|T_y\\xi(y)\\|^2d\\nu\\le C^2\\int\\|\\xi(y)\\|^2d\\nu\\)<\/span>, so the rule defines a bounded operator commuting with the scalar diagonal. Its entries are the original <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(M_{t_{ij}}\\)\">\\(M_{t_{ij}}\\)<\/span>. In particular this construction recovers the preceding <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(T\\)\">\\(T\\)<\/span>, since matrix entries determine a bounded operator. It proves equality of the operator norm with the essential supremum: one inequality is the integral bound, and the other follows by applying (A11) to the resulting operator. The function <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(y\\mapsto\\|T_y\\|\\)\">\\(y\\mapsto\\|T_y\\|\\)<\/span> is measurable, as the supremum of countably many normalized rational-vector tests.<\/p>\n<p>Pointwise adjoints have conjugate-transposed entries. Products act pointwise by composition on every vector field; their entries are the convergent sums <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(\\sum_k t_{ik}(y)s_{kj}(y)\\)\">\\(\\sum_k t_{ik}(y)s_{kj}(y)\\)<\/span>, whose absolute convergence follows from Cauchy–Schwarz for the two <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(\\ell^2\\)\">\\(\\ell^2\\)<\/span> rows and columns. Positivity is also pointwise: countably many rational quadratic-form tests give one common set on which <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(T_y\\ge0\\)\">\\(T_y\\ge0\\)<\/span> when <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(T\\ge0\\)\">\\(T\\ge0\\)<\/span>, and the converse follows by integration. Equality of two operators is equality of their countably many entries outside one common null set, hence equality of their fields there.<\/p>\n<p>Finally let <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(P_n=1\\otimes p_n\\)\">\\(P_n=1\\otimes p_n\\)<\/span>, where <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(p_n\\)\">\\(p_n\\)<\/span> projects onto the first <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(n\\)\">\\(n\\)<\/span> basis vectors, and take <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(p_n=1\\)\">\\(p_n=1\\)<\/span> once a finite basis is exhausted. Then\n<span class=\"math display\" data-tex=\"\\[\n P_nTP_n=\\sum_{i,j\\le n}M_{t_{ij}}\\otimes|e_i\\rangle\\langle e_j|\n       \\longrightarrow T\\quad\\text{strongly, together with adjoints}.\n \\tag{A12}\n\\]\">\\[\n P_nTP_n=\\sum_{i,j\\le n}M_{t_{ij}}\\otimes|e_i\\rangle\\langle e_j|\n       \\longrightarrow T\\quad\\text{strongly, together with adjoints}.\n \\tag{A12}\n\\]<\/span>\nThe bounds are <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(\\|P_nTP_n\\|\\le\\|T\\|\\)\">\\(\\|P_nTP_n\\|\\le\\|T\\|\\)<\/span>; convergence follows by expanding\n<span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(P_nT(P_n-1)\\xi+(P_n-1)T\\xi\\)\">\\(P_nT(P_n-1)\\xi+(P_n-1)T\\xi\\)<\/span>, and likewise for <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(T^*\\)\">\\(T^*\\)<\/span>. Thus <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(T\\in\\mathcal D\\)\">\\(T\\in\\mathcal D\\)<\/span>. The reverse inclusion follows from commutation of the tensor generators with the scalar diagonal. This proves all assertions. <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(\\square\\)\">\\(\\square\\)<\/span> The same proof applies to any nonzero separable Hilbert space in place of <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(H_0\\)\">\\(H_0\\)<\/span>; its only Hilbert-space input was a finite or countable orthonormal basis.<\/p>\n<p>Define measurable unitary fields\n<span class=\"math display\" data-tex=\"\\[\n U_n(y)=R_G(h_n(y)).\n \\tag{A13}\n\\]\">\\[\n U_n(y)=R_G(h_n(y)).\n \\tag{A13}\n\\]<\/span>\nTheir entries are Borel, since the selectors are Borel and <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(R_G\\)\">\\(R_G\\)<\/span> is strongly continuous; the lemma gives unitaries <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(U_n\\in\\mathcal D\\)\">\\(U_n\\in\\mathcal D\\)<\/span>. Put\n<span class=\"math display\" data-tex=\"\\[\n \\begin{aligned}\n \\mathcal D_H\n &amp;=\\mathcal D\\cap\\{U_n:n\\ge1\\}&#x27;\\\\\n &amp;=\\{T\\in\\mathcal D:\n       T_yR_G(h)=R_G(h)T_y\\ (h\\in H_y)\n       \\text{ for almost every }y\\}.\n \\end{aligned}\n \\tag{A14}\n\\]\">\\[\n \\begin{aligned}\n \\mathcal D_H\n &amp;=\\mathcal D\\cap\\{U_n:n\\ge1\\}&#x27;\\\\\n &amp;=\\{T\\in\\mathcal D:\n       T_yR_G(h)=R_G(h)T_y\\ (h\\in H_y)\n       \\text{ for almost every }y\\}.\n \\end{aligned}\n \\tag{A14}\n\\]<\/span>\nThe second equality has a precise common-null-set meaning. For <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(T\\)\">\\(T\\)<\/span> in the first algebra, the countably many equations <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(TU_n=U_nT\\)\">\\(TU_n=U_nT\\)<\/span> and the matrix lemma give one conull Borel set on which all corresponding field equations hold. For each <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(y\\)\">\\(y\\)<\/span> there, density of <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(\\{h_n(y)\\}\\)\">\\(\\{h_n(y)\\}\\)<\/span> in <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(H_y\\)\">\\(H_y\\)<\/span> and strong continuity extend commutation to every <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(h\\in H_y\\)\">\\(h\\in H_y\\)<\/span>. The converse is immediate. The first line makes <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(\\mathcal D_H\\)\">\\(\\mathcal D_H\\)<\/span> a von Neumann algebra, as an intersection of commutants; no interchange of a fiber commutant and a direct integral has been used.<\/p>\n","source":"docs/courses/OA-FLOW/src/OA-FLOW-L74.md","start_offset":12821,"end_offset":18318,"role":"registered_statement_and_proof_passage"},"9e731f0d5d2d488b8b084042a7c6cbf863c5bf179eaaf84d4f401995ac7f1f44":{"markdown":"# Covariant representations over a moving diagonal\n\n\n\nA diagonal algebra assigns scalar coordinates to a Hilbert space. A unitary\nnormalizing that algebra can move those coordinates, so its disintegration has\ntwo ingredients: a unitary between the appropriate fibres and a positive scalar\nthat compensates for the change of measure. We first construct this description\nfor a given covariant representation. We then build a different representation\nfrom a measure on the state space and prove exactly when its Hilbert integral is\nthe GNS space of the barycentre.\n\n<a id=\"oa-flow.covrep.setting\"><\/a>\n## Two constructions and their precise conclusions\n\nLet $A$ be a separable, possibly nonunital C*-algebra, and let $G$ be a separable\nlocally compact Hausdorff group. Let $\\alpha:G\\to\\operatorname{Aut}(A)$ be\npoint-norm continuous. Suppose $\\pi:A\\to B(H)$ is a nondegenerate representation\non a separable Hilbert space, $U:G\\to\\mathcal U(H)$ is strongly continuous, and\n\n$$U_g\\pi(a)U_g^*=\\pi(\\alpha_g(a)).\\tag{T1}$$\n\nFix a unital abelian von Neumann algebra $D\\subseteq\\pi(A)'$ satisfying\n$U_gDU_g^*=D$. We do not assume $D\\subseteq\\pi(A)''$. The zero Hilbert space\nhas an empty direct integral; below we give the construction for $H\\ne0$.\n\nThere are a compact metrizable space $X$, a continuous action $T:G\\times X\\to X$,\na full-support nonsingular probability $\\mu$, a measurable field of separable\nHilbert spaces $K_x$, and a unitary\n\n$$V:H\\longrightarrow\\mathcal K:=\\int_X^\\oplus K_x\\,d\\mu(x)$$\n\nfor which\n\n$$VDV^*=\\{M_f:f\\in L^\\infty(X,\\mu)\\},\\qquad\nV\\pi(a)V^*=\\int_X^\\oplus\\pi_x(a)\\,d\\mu(x).\\tag{T2}$$\n\nThe representations $\\pi_x$ are nondegenerate almost everywhere. For each fixed\n$g$ there are measurable unitary arrows $u(g,x):K_x\\to K_{T_gx}$, defined almost\neverywhere, such that, with $j_g=d((T_g)_*\\mu)/d\\mu$,\n\n$$ (VU_gV^*\\eta)(y)\n=j_g(y)^{1/2}u(g,T_g^{-1}y)\\eta(T_g^{-1}y).\\tag{T3}$$\n\nFor each fixed pair $g,h$ the identity\n\n$$u(gh,x)=u(g,T_hx)u(h,x)\\tag{T4}$$\n\nholds almost everywhere. For each fixed $g$, one conull set works for all $a\\in A$\nin\n\n$$u(g,x)\\pi_x(a)u(g,x)^*=\\pi_{T_gx}(\\alpha_g(a)).\\tag{T5}$$\n\nThese are fixed-parameter assertions. They do not assert one common invariant\nconull set for every group parameter. The subsequent state-space construction\nhas canonical arrows at every state and an exact cocycle there; its stronger\npointwise conclusion comes from that additional construction.\n\nEvery operator of $\\pi(A)''$ becomes a measurable field in the fibre algebras\n$\\pi_x(A)''$. The converse inclusion can fail: the two algebras in question are\nnot identified merely by (T2). An explicit constant-matrix example below will\nseparate them.\n\nFor the second construction let $\\nu$ be a quasi-invariant Borel probability\non the weak-star state space $S(A)$. The canonical GNS spaces over $S(A)$ carry\nan explicitly defined weighted, strongly continuous covariant representation.\nWe prove the following exact criterion: its canonical isometric embedding from\nthe GNS space of $\\varphi=\\int\\omega\\,d\\nu(\\omega)$ is onto if and only if,\nfor every Borel set $F$, the two partial barycentres\n$\\int_F\\omega\\,d\\nu$ and $\\int_{F^c}\\omega\\,d\\nu$ have no nonzero common\npositive minorant. This is the orthogonality convention used here; it is weaker\nthan norm orthogonality of the two functionals.\n\nThe proofs use the previously constructed\n[sequential approximate identity](OA-FLOW-L40.md#oa-flow.istate.approximation),\n[nonunital GNS representation](OA-FLOW-GNS.md#gns-theorem-5-1),\n[continuous functional calculus](OA-FLOW-CF.md#oa-flow.cf.6), and\n[scalar monotone convergence](OA-FLOW-SC.md#sc-04).\nExact field, measure and operator-topology inputs are linked where they enter.","html":"<h1>Covariant representations over a moving diagonal<\/h1>\n<p>A diagonal algebra assigns scalar coordinates to a Hilbert space. A unitary\nnormalizing that algebra can move those coordinates, so its disintegration has\ntwo ingredients: a unitary between the appropriate fibres and a positive scalar\nthat compensates for the change of measure. We first construct this description\nfor a given covariant representation. We then build a different representation\nfrom a measure on the state space and prove exactly when its Hilbert integral is\nthe GNS space of the barycentre.<\/p>\n<h2>Two constructions and their precise conclusions<\/h2>\n<p>Let <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(A\\)\">\\(A\\)<\/span> be a separable, possibly nonunital C*-algebra, and let <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(G\\)\">\\(G\\)<\/span> be a separable\nlocally compact Hausdorff group. Let <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(\\alpha:G\\to\\operatorname{Aut}(A)\\)\">\\(\\alpha:G\\to\\operatorname{Aut}(A)\\)<\/span> be\npoint-norm continuous. Suppose <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(\\pi:A\\to B(H)\\)\">\\(\\pi:A\\to B(H)\\)<\/span> is a nondegenerate representation\non a separable Hilbert space, <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(U:G\\to\\mathcal U(H)\\)\">\\(U:G\\to\\mathcal U(H)\\)<\/span> is strongly continuous, and<\/p>\n<p><span class=\"math display\" data-tex=\"\\[U_g\\pi(a)U_g^*=\\pi(\\alpha_g(a)).\\tag{T1}\\]\">\\[U_g\\pi(a)U_g^*=\\pi(\\alpha_g(a)).\\tag{T1}\\]<\/span><\/p>\n<p>Fix a unital abelian von Neumann algebra <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(D\\subseteq\\pi(A)&#x27;\\)\">\\(D\\subseteq\\pi(A)&#x27;\\)<\/span> satisfying\n<span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(U_gDU_g^*=D\\)\">\\(U_gDU_g^*=D\\)<\/span>. We do not assume <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(D\\subseteq\\pi(A)&#x27;&#x27;\\)\">\\(D\\subseteq\\pi(A)&#x27;&#x27;\\)<\/span>. The zero Hilbert space\nhas an empty direct integral; below we give the construction for <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(H\\ne0\\)\">\\(H\\ne0\\)<\/span>.<\/p>\n<p>There are a compact metrizable space <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(X\\)\">\\(X\\)<\/span>, a continuous action <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(T:G\\times X\\to X\\)\">\\(T:G\\times X\\to X\\)<\/span>,\na full-support nonsingular probability <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(\\mu\\)\">\\(\\mu\\)<\/span>, a measurable field of separable\nHilbert spaces <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(K_x\\)\">\\(K_x\\)<\/span>, and a unitary<\/p>\n<p><span class=\"math display\" data-tex=\"\\[V:H\\longrightarrow\\mathcal K:=\\int_X^\\oplus K_x\\,d\\mu(x)\\]\">\\[V:H\\longrightarrow\\mathcal K:=\\int_X^\\oplus K_x\\,d\\mu(x)\\]<\/span><\/p>\n<p>for which<\/p>\n<p><span class=\"math display\" data-tex=\"\\[VDV^*=\\{M_f:f\\in L^\\infty(X,\\mu)\\},\\qquad\nV\\pi(a)V^*=\\int_X^\\oplus\\pi_x(a)\\,d\\mu(x).\\tag{T2}\\]\">\\[VDV^*=\\{M_f:f\\in L^\\infty(X,\\mu)\\},\\qquad\nV\\pi(a)V^*=\\int_X^\\oplus\\pi_x(a)\\,d\\mu(x).\\tag{T2}\\]<\/span><\/p>\n<p>The representations <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(\\pi_x\\)\">\\(\\pi_x\\)<\/span> are nondegenerate almost everywhere. For each fixed\n<span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(g\\)\">\\(g\\)<\/span> there are measurable unitary arrows <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(u(g,x):K_x\\to K_{T_gx}\\)\">\\(u(g,x):K_x\\to K_{T_gx}\\)<\/span>, defined almost\neverywhere, such that, with <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(j_g=d((T_g)_*\\mu)/d\\mu\\)\">\\(j_g=d((T_g)_*\\mu)/d\\mu\\)<\/span>,<\/p>\n<p><span class=\"math display\" data-tex=\"\\[ (VU_gV^*\\eta)(y)\n=j_g(y)^{1/2}u(g,T_g^{-1}y)\\eta(T_g^{-1}y).\\tag{T3}\\]\">\\[ (VU_gV^*\\eta)(y)\n=j_g(y)^{1/2}u(g,T_g^{-1}y)\\eta(T_g^{-1}y).\\tag{T3}\\]<\/span><\/p>\n<p>For each fixed pair <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(g,h\\)\">\\(g,h\\)<\/span> the identity<\/p>\n<p><span class=\"math display\" data-tex=\"\\[u(gh,x)=u(g,T_hx)u(h,x)\\tag{T4}\\]\">\\[u(gh,x)=u(g,T_hx)u(h,x)\\tag{T4}\\]<\/span><\/p>\n<p>holds almost everywhere. For each fixed <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(g\\)\">\\(g\\)<\/span>, one conull set works for all <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(a\\in A\\)\">\\(a\\in A\\)<\/span>\nin<\/p>\n<p><span class=\"math display\" data-tex=\"\\[u(g,x)\\pi_x(a)u(g,x)^*=\\pi_{T_gx}(\\alpha_g(a)).\\tag{T5}\\]\">\\[u(g,x)\\pi_x(a)u(g,x)^*=\\pi_{T_gx}(\\alpha_g(a)).\\tag{T5}\\]<\/span><\/p>\n<p>These are fixed-parameter assertions. They do not assert one common invariant\nconull set for every group parameter. The subsequent state-space construction\nhas canonical arrows at every state and an exact cocycle there; its stronger\npointwise conclusion comes from that additional construction.<\/p>\n<p>Every operator of <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(\\pi(A)&#x27;&#x27;\\)\">\\(\\pi(A)&#x27;&#x27;\\)<\/span> becomes a measurable field in the fibre algebras\n<span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(\\pi_x(A)&#x27;&#x27;\\)\">\\(\\pi_x(A)&#x27;&#x27;\\)<\/span>. The converse inclusion can fail: the two algebras in question are\nnot identified merely by (T2). An explicit constant-matrix example below will\nseparate them.<\/p>\n<p>For the second construction let <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(\\nu\\)\">\\(\\nu\\)<\/span> be a quasi-invariant Borel probability\non the weak-star state space <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(S(A)\\)\">\\(S(A)\\)<\/span>. The canonical GNS spaces over <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(S(A)\\)\">\\(S(A)\\)<\/span> carry\nan explicitly defined weighted, strongly continuous covariant representation.\nWe prove the following exact criterion: its canonical isometric embedding from\nthe GNS space of <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(\\varphi=\\int\\omega\\,d\\nu(\\omega)\\)\">\\(\\varphi=\\int\\omega\\,d\\nu(\\omega)\\)<\/span> is onto if and only if,\nfor every Borel set <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(F\\)\">\\(F\\)<\/span>, the two partial barycentres\n<span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(\\int_F\\omega\\,d\\nu\\)\">\\(\\int_F\\omega\\,d\\nu\\)<\/span> and <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(\\int_{F^c}\\omega\\,d\\nu\\)\">\\(\\int_{F^c}\\omega\\,d\\nu\\)<\/span> have no nonzero common\npositive minorant. This is the orthogonality convention used here; it is weaker\nthan norm orthogonality of the two functionals.<\/p>\n<p>The proofs use the previously constructed\n<a href=\"../courses/OA-FLOW/OA-FLOW-L40.html#oa-flow.istate.approximation\">sequential approximate identity<\/a>,\n<a href=\"../courses/OA-FLOW/OA-FLOW-GNS.html#gns-theorem-5-1\">nonunital GNS representation<\/a>,\n<a href=\"../courses/OA-FLOW/OA-FLOW-CF.html#oa-flow.cf.6\">continuous functional calculus<\/a>, and\n<a href=\"../courses/OA-FLOW/OA-FLOW-SC.html#sc-04\">scalar monotone convergence<\/a>.\nExact field, measure and operator-topology inputs are linked where they enter.<\/p>\n","source":"docs/courses/OA-FLOW/src/OA-FLOW-L39.md","start_offset":0,"end_offset":3699,"role":"registered_statement_and_proof_passage"},"9eb7d12fdebdefd005eab4262afa33ab3bf3793764d4af60287d3132a78e9b08":{"markdown":"<a id=\"cbd-figure\"><\/a>\n## 8. The exact map and its sign\n\nThe following diagram is the actual commuting square (CBD20), with all four units shown. Its interval panel uses \\(s=1/4\\) in (CBD40), so the carrier interval moves from \\([0,1]\\) to \\([-1/4,3/4]\\). Intersecting the moved carrier with the original \\(q_\\phi\\) and applying \\(\\kappa_\\phi^{-1}\\) gives \\(E_{\\log h}([0,3/4])\\). This is the bounded evaluation map, not an assertion that \\(q_\\phi\\) itself is scaling-fixed.\n\n![Carrier evaluation commutes with density calculus, with exact translated intervals.](../assets/carrier-density-action/cbd-maps.svg)\n\nActual normal maps, (CBD20) and (CBD25); exact carrier and spectral intervals, (CBD40)–(CBD41). All units, the support compression and the translation sign are shown. The mathematical antecedents are Takesaki, *Theory of Operator Algebras II*, XII.4.1–4.4(i), pp. 403–405, and Exercise XII.4.1(d), p. 420. The displayed square and exact interval model are proved here. Original figure: CC0.","html":"<h2>8. The exact map and its sign<\/h2>\n<p>The following diagram is the actual commuting square (CBD20), with all four units shown. Its interval panel uses <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(s=1/4\\)\">\\(s=1/4\\)<\/span> in (CBD40), so the carrier interval moves from <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\([0,1]\\)\">\\([0,1]\\)<\/span> to <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\([-1/4,3/4]\\)\">\\([-1/4,3/4]\\)<\/span>. Intersecting the moved carrier with the original <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(q_\\phi\\)\">\\(q_\\phi\\)<\/span> and applying <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(\\kappa_\\phi^{-1}\\)\">\\(\\kappa_\\phi^{-1}\\)<\/span> gives <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(E_{\\log h}([0,3/4])\\)\">\\(E_{\\log h}([0,3/4])\\)<\/span>. This is the bounded evaluation map, not an assertion that <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(q_\\phi\\)\">\\(q_\\phi\\)<\/span> itself is scaling-fixed.<\/p>\n<p><a class=\"figure-link\" href=\"../courses/OA-FLOW/assets/carrier-density-action/cbd-maps.svg\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\"><img src=\"../courses/OA-FLOW/assets/carrier-density-action/cbd-maps.svg\" alt=\"Carrier evaluation commutes with density calculus, with exact translated intervals.\" /><span>Open figure at full size<\/span><\/a><\/p>\n<p>Actual normal maps, (CBD20) and (CBD25); exact carrier and spectral intervals, (CBD40)–(CBD41). All units, the support compression and the translation sign are shown. The mathematical antecedents are Takesaki, <em>Theory of Operator Algebras II<\/em>, XII.4.1–4.4(i), pp. 403–405, and Exercise XII.4.1(d), p. 420. The displayed square and exact interval model are proved here. Original figure: CC0.<\/p>\n","source":"docs/courses/OA-FLOW/src/OA-FLOW-CBD.md","start_offset":31083,"end_offset":32085,"role":"registered_statement_and_proof_passage"},"9ed614e0be54c007509ab331e777e84bc7ac762874c74fd7c0ab332f3cb24668":{"markdown":"# Entropy at return times\n\nThe entropy produced by one return to a set must be compared with the mean number of original steps in that return. Keeping the terminal symbol of each return word makes that comparison exact, even when return times are unbounded.\n\n<a id=\"IE-setting\"><\/a>\n## The return theorem and its scope\n\nLet \\((X,\\Sigma,\\mu)\\) be a probability space and let \\(T\\) be an invertible bimeasurable probability-preserving transformation. Assume it is ergodic: every measurable set invariant modulo null sets has measure zero or one. Let \\(E\\in\\Sigma\\) have measure \\(a>0\\). On the recurrent conull part of \\(E\\), write\n\\[\n r(x)=\\min\\{n\\ge1:T^nx\\in E\\},\\qquad\n Sx=T^{r(x)}x,\\qquad\n \\nu(B)=\\frac{\\mu(B)}a\\quad(B\\subseteq E).\n \\tag{IE1}\n\\]\nAll sigma algebras below are completed for their indicated measures. All logarithms are natural, and \\(0\\log0=0\\).\n\nFor a finite measurable partition \\(\\mathcal P\\), define\n\\[\n \\begin{aligned}\n H_\\mu(\\mathcal P)&=-\\sum_{P\\in\\mathcal P}\\mu(P)\\log\\mu(P),\\\\\n h_\\mu(T,\\mathcal P)&=\\lim_{n\\to\\infty}\\frac1n\n       H_\\mu\\!\\left(\\bigvee_{j=0}^{n-1}T^{-j}\\mathcal P\\right),\\\\\n h_\\mu(T)&=\\sup_{\\mathcal P\\ {\\rm finite}}h_\\mu(T,\\mathcal P).\n \\end{aligned}\n \\tag{IE2}\n\\]\nThe limit is proved below. Here \\(T^{-j}\\mathcal P\\) records the label \\(\\mathcal P(T^jx)\\).\n\n**Theorem.** The induced map \\(S\\) is an invertible ergodic probability-preserving transformation of \\((E,\\nu)\\), and\n\\[\n \\boxed{\\quad h_\\nu(S)=\\frac{h_\\mu(T)}{\\mu(E)}\n             \\quad\\text{in }[0,\\infty].\\quad}\n \\tag{IE3}\n\\]\nNeither nonatomicity nor aperiodicity is required. The return time is finite almost everywhere and has mean \\(1/a\\), but need not be bounded. No finite generating partition is assumed. The scalar integration and Hilbert-space inputs are proved in [SC2–7](OA-FLOW-SC.md#sc-02), [CF8](OA-FLOW-CF.md#oa-flow.cf.8) and [HR2–5](OA-FLOW-HR.md#hr-02). The proof works on the stated probability spaces; the standard probability spaces needed for the classification application are included.\n\nThe induction identity is due to L. M. Abramov. We prove it here from scalar integration, Hilbert projection and finite probability tables. In particular no ergodic theorem for time averages, entropy generator theorem, martingale convergence theorem, or factor-classification theorem is used as an unproved input.","html":"<h1>Entropy at return times<\/h1>\n<p>The entropy produced by one return to a set must be compared with the mean number of original steps in that return. Keeping the terminal symbol of each return word makes that comparison exact, even when return times are unbounded.<\/p>\n<h2>The return theorem and its scope<\/h2>\n<p>Let <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\((X,\\Sigma,\\mu)\\)\">\\((X,\\Sigma,\\mu)\\)<\/span> be a probability space and let <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(T\\)\">\\(T\\)<\/span> be an invertible bimeasurable probability-preserving transformation. Assume it is ergodic: every measurable set invariant modulo null sets has measure zero or one. Let <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(E\\in\\Sigma\\)\">\\(E\\in\\Sigma\\)<\/span> have measure <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(a&gt;0\\)\">\\(a&gt;0\\)<\/span>. On the recurrent conull part of <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(E\\)\">\\(E\\)<\/span>, write\n<span class=\"math display\" data-tex=\"\\[\n r(x)=\\min\\{n\\ge1:T^nx\\in E\\},\\qquad\n Sx=T^{r(x)}x,\\qquad\n \\nu(B)=\\frac{\\mu(B)}a\\quad(B\\subseteq E).\n \\tag{IE1}\n\\]\">\\[\n r(x)=\\min\\{n\\ge1:T^nx\\in E\\},\\qquad\n Sx=T^{r(x)}x,\\qquad\n \\nu(B)=\\frac{\\mu(B)}a\\quad(B\\subseteq E).\n \\tag{IE1}\n\\]<\/span>\nAll sigma algebras below are completed for their indicated measures. All logarithms are natural, and <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(0\\log0=0\\)\">\\(0\\log0=0\\)<\/span>.<\/p>\n<p>For a finite measurable partition <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(\\mathcal P\\)\">\\(\\mathcal P\\)<\/span>, define\n<span class=\"math display\" data-tex=\"\\[\n \\begin{aligned}\n H_\\mu(\\mathcal P)&amp;=-\\sum_{P\\in\\mathcal P}\\mu(P)\\log\\mu(P),\\\\\n h_\\mu(T,\\mathcal P)&amp;=\\lim_{n\\to\\infty}\\frac1n\n       H_\\mu\\!\\left(\\bigvee_{j=0}^{n-1}T^{-j}\\mathcal P\\right),\\\\\n h_\\mu(T)&amp;=\\sup_{\\mathcal P\\ {\\rm finite}}h_\\mu(T,\\mathcal P).\n \\end{aligned}\n \\tag{IE2}\n\\]\">\\[\n \\begin{aligned}\n H_\\mu(\\mathcal P)&amp;=-\\sum_{P\\in\\mathcal P}\\mu(P)\\log\\mu(P),\\\\\n h_\\mu(T,\\mathcal P)&amp;=\\lim_{n\\to\\infty}\\frac1n\n       H_\\mu\\!\\left(\\bigvee_{j=0}^{n-1}T^{-j}\\mathcal P\\right),\\\\\n h_\\mu(T)&amp;=\\sup_{\\mathcal P\\ {\\rm finite}}h_\\mu(T,\\mathcal P).\n \\end{aligned}\n \\tag{IE2}\n\\]<\/span>\nThe limit is proved below. Here <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(T^{-j}\\mathcal P\\)\">\\(T^{-j}\\mathcal P\\)<\/span> records the label <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(\\mathcal P(T^jx)\\)\">\\(\\mathcal P(T^jx)\\)<\/span>.<\/p>\n<p><strong>Theorem.<\/strong> The induced map <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(S\\)\">\\(S\\)<\/span> is an invertible ergodic probability-preserving transformation of <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\((E,\\nu)\\)\">\\((E,\\nu)\\)<\/span>, and\n<span class=\"math display\" data-tex=\"\\[\n \\boxed{\\quad h_\\nu(S)=\\frac{h_\\mu(T)}{\\mu(E)}\n             \\quad\\text{in }[0,\\infty].\\quad}\n \\tag{IE3}\n\\]\">\\[\n \\boxed{\\quad h_\\nu(S)=\\frac{h_\\mu(T)}{\\mu(E)}\n             \\quad\\text{in }[0,\\infty].\\quad}\n \\tag{IE3}\n\\]<\/span>\nNeither nonatomicity nor aperiodicity is required. The return time is finite almost everywhere and has mean <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(1/a\\)\">\\(1/a\\)<\/span>, but need not be bounded. No finite generating partition is assumed. The scalar integration and Hilbert-space inputs are proved in <a href=\"../courses/OA-FLOW/OA-FLOW-SC.html#sc-02\">SC2–7<\/a>, <a href=\"../courses/OA-FLOW/OA-FLOW-CF.html#oa-flow.cf.8\">CF8<\/a> and <a href=\"../courses/OA-FLOW/OA-FLOW-HR.html#hr-02\">HR2–5<\/a>. The proof works on the stated probability spaces; the standard probability spaces needed for the classification application are included.<\/p>\n<p>The induction identity is due to L. M. Abramov. We prove it here from scalar integration, Hilbert projection and finite probability tables. In particular no ergodic theorem for time averages, entropy generator theorem, martingale convergence theorem, or factor-classification theorem is used as an unproved input.<\/p>\n","source":"docs/courses/OA-FLOW/src/OA-FLOW-IE.md","start_offset":0,"end_offset":2327,"role":"registered_statement_and_proof_passage"},"9e4af975a16680d9e99753efa502276b81a5cad7b820d35fc29b78f722d5ad57":{"markdown":"<a id=\"ha-diagnostics\"><\/a>\n## 8. Six solved checks\n\n**1. Is the invariant-mean retraction normal?** In general it is not. For the shift on \\(\\ell^\\infty(\\mathbb Z)\\), invariance assigns the same mean value \\(t\\) to every singleton projection. The inequalities \\(nt\\le1\\) force \\(t=0\\). Every finite-interval projection has mean zero, though these projections increase to one. This proves failure of normality. Injectivity permits such a retraction. Every isomorphism in (HA15), (HA19), (HA20) and (HA23) is normal for the separately proved unitary reason.\n\n**2. Where does a four-position tensor go?** For inputs \\(a_1\\otimes a_2\\) and \\(b_1\\otimes b_2\\), interleaving gives \\(a_1\\otimes b_1\\otimes a_2\\otimes b_2\\). The inverse extracts positions \\(1,3\\) and \\(2,4\\). Its normalized trace is the product of the four normalized matrix traces, exactly the product of the two input traces. Interleaving introduces no factor of two.\n\n**3. Does the infinite trace hide the normalization?** Let \\(p,q\\) be rank-one projections in the first \\(M_2\\) positions of the two copies of \\(R\\), and let \\(e\\in B(K)\\) have rank \\(k\\). Before absorption the trace of \\(p\\otimes e\\otimes q\\) is \\(k/4\\). After absorption \\(J(p\\otimes q)\\) has normalized trace \\(1/4\\), so its tensor with \\(e\\) again has trace \\(k/4\\). These finite tests and (HA21) fix the scale despite infinite trace of the unit.\n\n**4. Can different section masses defeat the entropy argument?** If \\(a_0=1/3\\), \\(a_1=1/4\\), the normalized return entropies are \\(0\\) and \\(4\\log2\\). In a point realization a hypothetical conjugacy carries the first unnormalized restriction to \\((a_0/a_1)\\mu_1|_{E_1}=(4/3)\\mu_1|_{E_1}\\). It preserves the normalized probabilities, so the entropy contradiction persists. Equal section mass was never required.\n\n**5. Does a copy of \\(R\\) inside \\(A_i\\) suffice?** No. Containment does not say that copy generates \\(A_i\\). The provider approximates every finite subset, then uses exact containment of successive dyadic approximants to a dense sequence. This makes the union in (HA13) generate the entire algebra. That density makes \\(V_i\\) onto and transports the whole left algebra in (HA15). A bare embedded hyperfinite subfactor would leave the decisive step unproved.\n\n**6. Could an unbounded density restore contraction?** The bands \\(\\{1/m\\le b\\le m\\}\\) exhaust almost all the probability space for every finite strictly positive measurable \\(b\\). One has positive measure, and recurrence there alone contradicts \\(1/m\\le c^n m\\). There is no global bound assumption. The density in (HA26) is \\(b\\circ T_i\\), not \\(b\\circ T_i^{-1}\\): change variables in \\(\\int b(x)f(T_i^{-1}x)\\,d\\mu_i(x)\\).","html":"<h2>8. Six solved checks<\/h2>\n<p><strong>1. Is the invariant-mean retraction normal?<\/strong> In general it is not. For the shift on <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(\\ell^\\infty(\\mathbb Z)\\)\">\\(\\ell^\\infty(\\mathbb Z)\\)<\/span>, invariance assigns the same mean value <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(t\\)\">\\(t\\)<\/span> to every singleton projection. The inequalities <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(nt\\le1\\)\">\\(nt\\le1\\)<\/span> force <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(t=0\\)\">\\(t=0\\)<\/span>. Every finite-interval projection has mean zero, though these projections increase to one. This proves failure of normality. Injectivity permits such a retraction. Every isomorphism in (HA15), (HA19), (HA20) and (HA23) is normal for the separately proved unitary reason.<\/p>\n<p><strong>2. Where does a four-position tensor go?<\/strong> For inputs <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(a_1\\otimes a_2\\)\">\\(a_1\\otimes a_2\\)<\/span> and <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(b_1\\otimes b_2\\)\">\\(b_1\\otimes b_2\\)<\/span>, interleaving gives <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(a_1\\otimes b_1\\otimes a_2\\otimes b_2\\)\">\\(a_1\\otimes b_1\\otimes a_2\\otimes b_2\\)<\/span>. The inverse extracts positions <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(1,3\\)\">\\(1,3\\)<\/span> and <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(2,4\\)\">\\(2,4\\)<\/span>. Its normalized trace is the product of the four normalized matrix traces, exactly the product of the two input traces. Interleaving introduces no factor of two.<\/p>\n<p><strong>3. Does the infinite trace hide the normalization?<\/strong> Let <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(p,q\\)\">\\(p,q\\)<\/span> be rank-one projections in the first <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(M_2\\)\">\\(M_2\\)<\/span> positions of the two copies of <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(R\\)\">\\(R\\)<\/span>, and let <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(e\\in B(K)\\)\">\\(e\\in B(K)\\)<\/span> have rank <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(k\\)\">\\(k\\)<\/span>. Before absorption the trace of <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(p\\otimes e\\otimes q\\)\">\\(p\\otimes e\\otimes q\\)<\/span> is <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(k/4\\)\">\\(k/4\\)<\/span>. After absorption <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(J(p\\otimes q)\\)\">\\(J(p\\otimes q)\\)<\/span> has normalized trace <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(1/4\\)\">\\(1/4\\)<\/span>, so its tensor with <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(e\\)\">\\(e\\)<\/span> again has trace <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(k/4\\)\">\\(k/4\\)<\/span>. These finite tests and (HA21) fix the scale despite infinite trace of the unit.<\/p>\n<p><strong>4. Can different section masses defeat the entropy argument?<\/strong> If <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(a_0=1/3\\)\">\\(a_0=1/3\\)<\/span>, <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(a_1=1/4\\)\">\\(a_1=1/4\\)<\/span>, the normalized return entropies are <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(0\\)\">\\(0\\)<\/span> and <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(4\\log2\\)\">\\(4\\log2\\)<\/span>. In a point realization a hypothetical conjugacy carries the first unnormalized restriction to <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\((a_0/a_1)\\mu_1|_{E_1}=(4/3)\\mu_1|_{E_1}\\)\">\\((a_0/a_1)\\mu_1|_{E_1}=(4/3)\\mu_1|_{E_1}\\)<\/span>. It preserves the normalized probabilities, so the entropy contradiction persists. Equal section mass was never required.<\/p>\n<p><strong>5. Does a copy of <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(R\\)\">\\(R\\)<\/span> inside <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(A_i\\)\">\\(A_i\\)<\/span> suffice?<\/strong> No. Containment does not say that copy generates <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(A_i\\)\">\\(A_i\\)<\/span>. The provider approximates every finite subset, then uses exact containment of successive dyadic approximants to a dense sequence. This makes the union in (HA13) generate the entire algebra. That density makes <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(V_i\\)\">\\(V_i\\)<\/span> onto and transports the whole left algebra in (HA15). A bare embedded hyperfinite subfactor would leave the decisive step unproved.<\/p>\n<p><strong>6. Could an unbounded density restore contraction?<\/strong> The bands <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(\\{1/m\\le b\\le m\\}\\)\">\\(\\{1/m\\le b\\le m\\}\\)<\/span> exhaust almost all the probability space for every finite strictly positive measurable <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(b\\)\">\\(b\\)<\/span>. One has positive measure, and recurrence there alone contradicts <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(1/m\\le c^n m\\)\">\\(1/m\\le c^n m\\)<\/span>. There is no global bound assumption. The density in (HA26) is <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(b\\circ T_i\\)\">\\(b\\circ T_i\\)<\/span>, not <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(b\\circ T_i^{-1}\\)\">\\(b\\circ T_i^{-1}\\)<\/span>: change variables in <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(\\int b(x)f(T_i^{-1}x)\\,d\\mu_i(x)\\)\">\\(\\int b(x)f(T_i^{-1}x)\\,d\\mu_i(x)\\)<\/span>.<\/p>\n","source":"docs/courses/OA-FLOW/src/OA-FLOW-HA.md","start_offset":30334,"end_offset":33015,"role":"registered_statement_and_proof_passage"},"9ed6fcb92d311b8e09d7fded60e1a70205d6f91b2a7a3be259a4e83c790c8cd3":{"markdown":"<a id=\"wo-model\"><\/a>\n## 7. Exact models and solved diagnostics\n\n**Semifinite density chart.** The examples use the faithful density weights and complete-domain criterion proved in [the trace specialization](#wo-trace-densities). Here \\(h,k\\) are the original trace densities. The same calculation supplies the commuting-density and matrix-distance models in the next lesson.\n\n**1. The half-depth test can stop before depth one.** On \\(M_2(\\mathbb C)\\), with its usual trace, let\n\\[\n h=\\begin{pmatrix}1&0\\\\0&2\\end{pmatrix},\\qquad\n k=\\begin{pmatrix}2&1\\\\1&3\\end{pmatrix},\\qquad\n \\varphi=\\operatorname{Tr}(h\\,\\cdot),\\quad\n \\psi=\\operatorname{Tr}(k\\,\\cdot).\n \\tag{WO24}\n\\]\nBoth are faithful. Since \\(k-h=\\begin{pmatrix}1&1\\\\1&1\\end{pmatrix}\\geq0\\), ordinary weight order and depth one-half hold. But\n\\[\n k^2-h^2=\\begin{pmatrix}4&5\\\\5&6\\end{pmatrix},\\qquad\n \\langle(k^2-h^2)(5,-4),(5,-4)\\rangle=-4.\n \\tag{WO25}\n\\]\nThus depth one fails. The forced lower endpoint sends \\(k(5,-4)=(6,-7)\\) to \\(h(5,-4)=(5,-8)\\); its squared norm increases from \\(85\\) to \\(89\\). This is an exact obstruction, not a numerical estimate of the largest possible strip.\n\n![The depth-one endpoint increases the squared norm of an exact vector from 85 to 89](../assets/weight-order/order-depth-obstruction.svg)\n\nThe real coordinate plane shows the exact obstruction in (WO24)–(WO25); the dashed circle has radius \\(\\sqrt{85}\\). This proves failure at depth one, without claiming the largest possible strip. [Numerical checks](../assets/weight-order/diagnostics.json); the proof is the exact matrix computation above.\n\n**2. All-depth order does not imply commutation.** Keep \\(h=\\operatorname{diag}(1,2)\\), but take \\(k=\\begin{pmatrix}4&1\\\\1&4\\end{pmatrix}\\). Its eigenvalues are \\(3,5\\). Thus \\(h^p\\leq2^p1\\leq3^p1\\leq k^p\\) for every \\(p>0\\), so \\(\\varphi\\preceq_\\infty\\psi\\). Nevertheless \\(hk-kh=\\begin{pmatrix}0&-1\\\\1&0\\end{pmatrix}\\ne0\\).\n\n**3. The scalar ratio fixes both signs.** If \\(\\varphi=c\\psi\\), then the unique extension is \\(c^{iz}1\\), of norm \\(c^a\\) at depth \\(a\\). Every positive depth works exactly when \\(c\\leq1\\). The two off-diagonal frequencies in (WO23) are \\(\\log c\\) and \\(-\\log c\\).\n\n**4. Why faithful densities do not need bounded inverses.** On \\(\\ell^2(\\mathbb N)\\), put \\(ke_{2n-1}=ne_{2n-1}\\), \\(ke_{2n}=n^{-1}e_{2n}\\), and \\(h=ck\\), \\(0<c<1\\). Both densities and both inverses are unbounded. Their trace weights are faithful normal semifinite by [TD6](OA-FLOW-TD.md#td-6). On the full inverse-power domain, \\(h^ak^{-a}=c^a1\\), whose closure is bounded. Thus the strip is \\(c^{iz}1\\) at every depth. None of the unbounded products is silently evaluated off its natural domain.\n\n**5. Infinite values are tested too.** Let \\(x\\in M_+\\) have \\(\\varphi(x)=\\infty\\). Formula (WO6) says \\(\\widetilde\\varphi(X_x)=\\infty\\). If the dual inequality holds, it forces \\(\\widetilde\\psi(X_x)=\\psi(x)=\\infty\\). Thus the order-reflection argument has not discarded the part of the cone on which the weights are infinite.\n\n**6. Why zero frequency is included.** A fixed nonzero \\(x\\) has constant extension \\(F(z)=x\\), and its spectral support is \\(\\{0\\}\\). The modulation-and-limit step after (WO17) is what permits this endpoint. Replacing either closed half-line in (WO23) by an open one would exclude equal weights.","html":"<h2>7. Exact models and solved diagnostics<\/h2>\n<p><strong>Semifinite density chart.<\/strong> The examples use the faithful density weights and complete-domain criterion proved in <a title=\"Reference location unresolved in this edition\" data-unresolved-reference=\"#wo-trace-densities\">the trace specialization<\/a>. Here <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(h,k\\)\">\\(h,k\\)<\/span> are the original trace densities. The same calculation supplies the commuting-density and matrix-distance models in the next lesson.<\/p>\n<p><strong>1. The half-depth test can stop before depth one.<\/strong> On <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(M_2(\\mathbb C)\\)\">\\(M_2(\\mathbb C)\\)<\/span>, with its usual trace, let\n<span class=\"math display\" data-tex=\"\\[\n h=\\begin{pmatrix}1&amp;0\\\\0&amp;2\\end{pmatrix},\\qquad\n k=\\begin{pmatrix}2&amp;1\\\\1&amp;3\\end{pmatrix},\\qquad\n \\varphi=\\operatorname{Tr}(h\\,\\cdot),\\quad\n \\psi=\\operatorname{Tr}(k\\,\\cdot).\n \\tag{WO24}\n\\]\">\\[\n h=\\begin{pmatrix}1&amp;0\\\\0&amp;2\\end{pmatrix},\\qquad\n k=\\begin{pmatrix}2&amp;1\\\\1&amp;3\\end{pmatrix},\\qquad\n \\varphi=\\operatorname{Tr}(h\\,\\cdot),\\quad\n \\psi=\\operatorname{Tr}(k\\,\\cdot).\n \\tag{WO24}\n\\]<\/span>\nBoth are faithful. Since <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(k-h=\\begin{pmatrix}1&amp;1\\\\1&amp;1\\end{pmatrix}\\geq0\\)\">\\(k-h=\\begin{pmatrix}1&amp;1\\\\1&amp;1\\end{pmatrix}\\geq0\\)<\/span>, ordinary weight order and depth one-half hold. But\n<span class=\"math display\" data-tex=\"\\[\n k^2-h^2=\\begin{pmatrix}4&amp;5\\\\5&amp;6\\end{pmatrix},\\qquad\n \\langle(k^2-h^2)(5,-4),(5,-4)\\rangle=-4.\n \\tag{WO25}\n\\]\">\\[\n k^2-h^2=\\begin{pmatrix}4&amp;5\\\\5&amp;6\\end{pmatrix},\\qquad\n \\langle(k^2-h^2)(5,-4),(5,-4)\\rangle=-4.\n \\tag{WO25}\n\\]<\/span>\nThus depth one fails. The forced lower endpoint sends <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(k(5,-4)=(6,-7)\\)\">\\(k(5,-4)=(6,-7)\\)<\/span> to <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(h(5,-4)=(5,-8)\\)\">\\(h(5,-4)=(5,-8)\\)<\/span>; its squared norm increases from <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(85\\)\">\\(85\\)<\/span> to <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(89\\)\">\\(89\\)<\/span>. This is an exact obstruction, not a numerical estimate of the largest possible strip.<\/p>\n<p><a class=\"figure-link\" href=\"../courses/OA-FLOW/assets/weight-order/order-depth-obstruction.svg\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\"><img src=\"../courses/OA-FLOW/assets/weight-order/order-depth-obstruction.svg\" alt=\"The depth-one endpoint increases the squared norm of an exact vector from 85 to 89\" /><span>Open figure at full size<\/span><\/a><\/p>\n<p>The real coordinate plane shows the exact obstruction in (WO24)–(WO25); the dashed circle has radius <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(\\sqrt{85}\\)\">\\(\\sqrt{85}\\)<\/span>. This proves failure at depth one, without claiming the largest possible strip. <a href=\"../courses/OA-FLOW/assets/weight-order/diagnostics.json\">Numerical checks<\/a>; the proof is the exact matrix computation above.<\/p>\n<p><strong>2. All-depth order does not imply commutation.<\/strong> Keep <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(h=\\operatorname{diag}(1,2)\\)\">\\(h=\\operatorname{diag}(1,2)\\)<\/span>, but take <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(k=\\begin{pmatrix}4&amp;1\\\\1&amp;4\\end{pmatrix}\\)\">\\(k=\\begin{pmatrix}4&amp;1\\\\1&amp;4\\end{pmatrix}\\)<\/span>. Its eigenvalues are <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(3,5\\)\">\\(3,5\\)<\/span>. Thus <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(h^p\\leq2^p1\\leq3^p1\\leq k^p\\)\">\\(h^p\\leq2^p1\\leq3^p1\\leq k^p\\)<\/span> for every <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(p&gt;0\\)\">\\(p&gt;0\\)<\/span>, so <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(\\varphi\\preceq_\\infty\\psi\\)\">\\(\\varphi\\preceq_\\infty\\psi\\)<\/span>. Nevertheless <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(hk-kh=\\begin{pmatrix}0&amp;-1\\\\1&amp;0\\end{pmatrix}\\ne0\\)\">\\(hk-kh=\\begin{pmatrix}0&amp;-1\\\\1&amp;0\\end{pmatrix}\\ne0\\)<\/span>.<\/p>\n<p><strong>3. The scalar ratio fixes both signs.<\/strong> If <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(\\varphi=c\\psi\\)\">\\(\\varphi=c\\psi\\)<\/span>, then the unique extension is <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(c^{iz}1\\)\">\\(c^{iz}1\\)<\/span>, of norm <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(c^a\\)\">\\(c^a\\)<\/span> at depth <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(a\\)\">\\(a\\)<\/span>. Every positive depth works exactly when <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(c\\leq1\\)\">\\(c\\leq1\\)<\/span>. The two off-diagonal frequencies in (WO23) are <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(\\log c\\)\">\\(\\log c\\)<\/span> and <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(-\\log c\\)\">\\(-\\log c\\)<\/span>.<\/p>\n<p><strong>4. Why faithful densities do not need bounded inverses.<\/strong> On <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(\\ell^2(\\mathbb N)\\)\">\\(\\ell^2(\\mathbb N)\\)<\/span>, put <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(ke_{2n-1}=ne_{2n-1}\\)\">\\(ke_{2n-1}=ne_{2n-1}\\)<\/span>, <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(ke_{2n}=n^{-1}e_{2n}\\)\">\\(ke_{2n}=n^{-1}e_{2n}\\)<\/span>, and <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(h=ck\\)\">\\(h=ck\\)<\/span>, <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(0&lt;c&lt;1\\)\">\\(0&lt;c&lt;1\\)<\/span>. Both densities and both inverses are unbounded. Their trace weights are faithful normal semifinite by <a href=\"../courses/OA-FLOW/OA-FLOW-TD.html#td-6\">TD6<\/a>. On the full inverse-power domain, <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(h^ak^{-a}=c^a1\\)\">\\(h^ak^{-a}=c^a1\\)<\/span>, whose closure is bounded. Thus the strip is <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(c^{iz}1\\)\">\\(c^{iz}1\\)<\/span> at every depth. None of the unbounded products is silently evaluated off its natural domain.<\/p>\n<p><strong>5. Infinite values are tested too.<\/strong> Let <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(x\\in M_+\\)\">\\(x\\in M_+\\)<\/span> have <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(\\varphi(x)=\\infty\\)\">\\(\\varphi(x)=\\infty\\)<\/span>. Formula (WO6) says <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(\\widetilde\\varphi(X_x)=\\infty\\)\">\\(\\widetilde\\varphi(X_x)=\\infty\\)<\/span>. If the dual inequality holds, it forces <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(\\widetilde\\psi(X_x)=\\psi(x)=\\infty\\)\">\\(\\widetilde\\psi(X_x)=\\psi(x)=\\infty\\)<\/span>. Thus the order-reflection argument has not discarded the part of the cone on which the weights are infinite.<\/p>\n<p><strong>6. Why zero frequency is included.<\/strong> A fixed nonzero <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(x\\)\">\\(x\\)<\/span> has constant extension <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(F(z)=x\\)\">\\(F(z)=x\\)<\/span>, and its spectral support is <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(\\{0\\}\\)\">\\(\\{0\\}\\)<\/span>. The modulation-and-limit step after (WO17) is what permits this endpoint. Replacing either closed half-line in (WO23) by an open one would exclude equal weights.<\/p>\n","source":"docs/courses/OA-FLOW/src/OA-FLOW-WORD.md","start_offset":21226,"end_offset":24530,"role":"registered_statement_and_proof_passage"},"9e0a32e21938a69a5b2cdb51d1bea6cd7b2e36b8564698e63c9f2df17acd7b6b":{"markdown":"<a id=\"ps-monodromy\"><\/a>\n## 2. Every periodic cocycle and its topology\n\nLet \\(A=\\mathcal U(L^\\infty(\\mathbb R/P\\mathbb Z))\\), with the action in (PS3). For a strongly continuous cocycle \\(c\\), commutativity of the two expressions for \\(c_{s+P}\\) gives\n\\[\n c_Pc_s=c_s\\theta_s(c_P).\n\\]\nThus \\(c_P\\) is invariant under all translations and is a scalar \\(\\zeta\\). Translation invariance implies constancy: convolve with continuous approximate identities on the circle; each convolution is a continuous invariant function, hence constant, and these converge in \\(L^2\\). Define its monodromy by\n\\[\n \\operatorname{Mon}(c)=c_P\\in\\mathbb T.\n \\tag{PS5}\n\\]\n\n**Complete cocycle classification.** Every \\(c\\) has the form\n\\[\n c_s(q)=e^{-its}\\,a(q)^*a(q-s)\n \\quad(s\\in\\mathbb R),\\qquad a\\in A,\\quad\n e^{-iPt}=\\operatorname{Mon}(c).\n \\tag{PS6}\n\\]\nThe real parameter \\(t\\) is unique modulo \\(T\\mathbb Z\\).\n\n*Proof.* Pull the periodic multiplier classes back to \\(L^\\infty(\\mathbb R,dq)\\). This is a normal injective homomorphism. Strong continuity is preserved: testing on a square-integrable vector reduces, by its periodized squared density, to normal-function testing on one period. The full line-translation transfer theorem [TCC3–5](OA-FLOW-TCC.md#tcc-3), reflected to negative translation and with its transfer replaced by its adjoint if necessary, gives\n\\[\n c_s(q)=b(q)^*b(q-s),\\qquad b\\in\\mathcal U(L^\\infty(\\mathbb R)).\n\\]\nThis is an all-times equality of multiplier classes. That theorem constructs the necessary joint measurable representatives and a generic Fubini slice; no evaluation of an \\(L^\\infty\\) class at \\(q=0\\) is involved.\n\nAt \\(s=P\\), the equality says \\(b(q-P)=\\zeta b(q)\\) almost everywhere. Choose \\(t\\) with \\(e^{-iPt}=\\zeta\\), and put \\(a(q)=e^{-itq}b(q)\\). Then \\(a(q-P)=a(q)\\) almost everywhere. Discarding the countable union of exceptional sets for integer translates gives a periodic representative obtained from its restriction to \\([0,P)\\). This defines \\(a\\in A\\). Direct cancellation yields (PS6) at every time in the multiplier algebra. If two choices \\(t,t'\\) occur, equality of monodromy gives \\(P(t-t')\\in2\\pi\\mathbb Z\\), equivalently \\(t-t'\\in T\\mathbb Z\\). ∎\n\nMonodromy is a continuous surjective homomorphism from the compact-open strong cocycle group onto \\(\\mathbb T\\). Continuity is evaluation at \\(P\\); on scalar unitaries strong convergence is their ordinary scalar convergence. Surjectivity follows from the scalar characters. Its kernel is precisely \\(B^1_\\theta(\\mathbb R,A)\\), by (PS6). The cocycle group is Polish by the path-space proof in [Characteristic, Section 4](OA-FLOW-NSQ.md#cs-topology). Polish open mapping therefore gives\n\\[\n H^1_\\theta(\\mathbb R,A)\\ \\cong\\ \\mathbb T\n \\ \\cong\\ \\mathbb R/T\\mathbb Z,\\qquad\n [c]\\longmapsto e^{-iPt},\\quad [t]\\longmapsto e^{-iPt}.\n \\tag{PS7}\n\\]\nIn particular the coboundaries are closed, and their quotient topology agrees with their subspace topology here. This last assertion follows also by open mapping from \\(A/\\mathbb T\\) onto the closed kernel.\n\nWrite \\(E\\) for the normalizers in the continuous core and \\(N_c=\\operatorname{Cntr}(M)\\). With the convention \\(\\delta(v)_s=v^*\\theta_s(v)\\), the full square is\n\\[\n\\begin{array}{ccccc}\n \\mathbb T&\\longrightarrow&A&\\longrightarrow&B^1_\\theta\\\\\n \\downarrow&&\\downarrow&&\\downarrow\\\\\n \\mathcal U(M)&\\longrightarrow&E&\\longrightarrow&Z^1_\\theta\\\\\n \\downarrow&&\\downarrow&&\\downarrow\\\\\n \\operatorname{Int}(M)&\\longrightarrow&N_c&\\xrightarrow{\\nu}&\\mathbb R/T\\mathbb Z .\n\\end{array}\n\\tag{PS8}\n\\]\nEvery row and column is exact with \\(1\\)'s at both ends, by the already proved general characteristic square and the actual identification (PS7). Explicitly, if \\(v\\in E\\), factor its defect by (PS6):\n\\(\\delta(v)=e^{-it\\,\\cdot}\\delta(a)\\).\nThen \\(v a^*\\lambda_\\varphi(t)^*\\) is fixed by \\(\\theta\\), hence belongs to \\(\\mathcal U(M)\\). Therefore\n\\[\n N_c=\\{\\operatorname{Ad}(w)\\sigma_t^\\varphi:\n               w\\in\\mathcal U(M),\\ t\\in\\mathbb R\\},\\qquad\n \\nu(\\operatorname{Ad}(w)\\sigma_t^\\varphi)=[t].\n \\tag{PS9}\n\\]\nThe kernel and independence of this formula follow from (PS7) and exactness, or from the actual inner-period theorem in DDP2. Thus the formula retains all extended inner automorphisms.\n\nThe \\(\\operatorname{Aut}(M)\\times\\mathbb R\\) action of the general square is retained. On the center, every canonical lift commutes with translation. Such an automorphism is a rotation: the eigenspace for the character \\(e^{-iTs}\\) consists of scalar multiples of \\(e^{iTq}\\), since the ratio of two such eigenunitaries is invariant. It therefore sends the generating unitary to a scalar multiple and hence acts by a unique translation modulo \\(P\\). Scalar monodromy is unchanged. Thus both acting groups act trivially on the last entry of (PS8), although their actions on \\(A,E\\) and the automorphism groups need not be trivial.","html":"<h2>2. Every periodic cocycle and its topology<\/h2>\n<p>Let <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(A=\\mathcal U(L^\\infty(\\mathbb R/P\\mathbb Z))\\)\">\\(A=\\mathcal U(L^\\infty(\\mathbb R/P\\mathbb Z))\\)<\/span>, with the action in (PS3). For a strongly continuous cocycle <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(c\\)\">\\(c\\)<\/span>, commutativity of the two expressions for <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(c_{s+P}\\)\">\\(c_{s+P}\\)<\/span> gives\n<span class=\"math display\" data-tex=\"\\[\n c_Pc_s=c_s\\theta_s(c_P).\n\\]\">\\[\n c_Pc_s=c_s\\theta_s(c_P).\n\\]<\/span>\nThus <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(c_P\\)\">\\(c_P\\)<\/span> is invariant under all translations and is a scalar <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(\\zeta\\)\">\\(\\zeta\\)<\/span>. Translation invariance implies constancy: convolve with continuous approximate identities on the circle; each convolution is a continuous invariant function, hence constant, and these converge in <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(L^2\\)\">\\(L^2\\)<\/span>. Define its monodromy by\n<span class=\"math display\" data-tex=\"\\[\n \\operatorname{Mon}(c)=c_P\\in\\mathbb T.\n \\tag{PS5}\n\\]\">\\[\n \\operatorname{Mon}(c)=c_P\\in\\mathbb T.\n \\tag{PS5}\n\\]<\/span><\/p>\n<p><strong>Complete cocycle classification.<\/strong> Every <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(c\\)\">\\(c\\)<\/span> has the form\n<span class=\"math display\" data-tex=\"\\[\n c_s(q)=e^{-its}\\,a(q)^*a(q-s)\n \\quad(s\\in\\mathbb R),\\qquad a\\in A,\\quad\n e^{-iPt}=\\operatorname{Mon}(c).\n \\tag{PS6}\n\\]\">\\[\n c_s(q)=e^{-its}\\,a(q)^*a(q-s)\n \\quad(s\\in\\mathbb R),\\qquad a\\in A,\\quad\n e^{-iPt}=\\operatorname{Mon}(c).\n \\tag{PS6}\n\\]<\/span>\nThe real parameter <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(t\\)\">\\(t\\)<\/span> is unique modulo <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(T\\mathbb Z\\)\">\\(T\\mathbb Z\\)<\/span>.<\/p>\n<p><em>Proof.<\/em> Pull the periodic multiplier classes back to <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(L^\\infty(\\mathbb R,dq)\\)\">\\(L^\\infty(\\mathbb R,dq)\\)<\/span>. This is a normal injective homomorphism. Strong continuity is preserved: testing on a square-integrable vector reduces, by its periodized squared density, to normal-function testing on one period. The full line-translation transfer theorem <a href=\"../courses/OA-FLOW/OA-FLOW-TCC.html#tcc-3\">TCC3–5<\/a>, reflected to negative translation and with its transfer replaced by its adjoint if necessary, gives\n<span class=\"math display\" data-tex=\"\\[\n c_s(q)=b(q)^*b(q-s),\\qquad b\\in\\mathcal U(L^\\infty(\\mathbb R)).\n\\]\">\\[\n c_s(q)=b(q)^*b(q-s),\\qquad b\\in\\mathcal U(L^\\infty(\\mathbb R)).\n\\]<\/span>\nThis is an all-times equality of multiplier classes. That theorem constructs the necessary joint measurable representatives and a generic Fubini slice; no evaluation of an <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(L^\\infty\\)\">\\(L^\\infty\\)<\/span> class at <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(q=0\\)\">\\(q=0\\)<\/span> is involved.<\/p>\n<p>At <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(s=P\\)\">\\(s=P\\)<\/span>, the equality says <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(b(q-P)=\\zeta b(q)\\)\">\\(b(q-P)=\\zeta b(q)\\)<\/span> almost everywhere. Choose <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(t\\)\">\\(t\\)<\/span> with <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(e^{-iPt}=\\zeta\\)\">\\(e^{-iPt}=\\zeta\\)<\/span>, and put <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(a(q)=e^{-itq}b(q)\\)\">\\(a(q)=e^{-itq}b(q)\\)<\/span>. Then <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(a(q-P)=a(q)\\)\">\\(a(q-P)=a(q)\\)<\/span> almost everywhere. Discarding the countable union of exceptional sets for integer translates gives a periodic representative obtained from its restriction to <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\([0,P)\\)\">\\([0,P)\\)<\/span>. This defines <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(a\\in A\\)\">\\(a\\in A\\)<\/span>. Direct cancellation yields (PS6) at every time in the multiplier algebra. If two choices <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(t,t&#x27;\\)\">\\(t,t&#x27;\\)<\/span> occur, equality of monodromy gives <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(P(t-t&#x27;)\\in2\\pi\\mathbb Z\\)\">\\(P(t-t&#x27;)\\in2\\pi\\mathbb Z\\)<\/span>, equivalently <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(t-t&#x27;\\in T\\mathbb Z\\)\">\\(t-t&#x27;\\in T\\mathbb Z\\)<\/span>. ∎<\/p>\n<p>Monodromy is a continuous surjective homomorphism from the compact-open strong cocycle group onto <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(\\mathbb T\\)\">\\(\\mathbb T\\)<\/span>. Continuity is evaluation at <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(P\\)\">\\(P\\)<\/span>; on scalar unitaries strong convergence is their ordinary scalar convergence. Surjectivity follows from the scalar characters. Its kernel is precisely <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(B^1_\\theta(\\mathbb R,A)\\)\">\\(B^1_\\theta(\\mathbb R,A)\\)<\/span>, by (PS6). The cocycle group is Polish by the path-space proof in <a href=\"../courses/OA-FLOW/OA-FLOW-NSQ.html#cs-topology\">Characteristic, Section 4<\/a>. Polish open mapping therefore gives\n<span class=\"math display\" data-tex=\"\\[\n H^1_\\theta(\\mathbb R,A)\\ \\cong\\ \\mathbb T\n \\ \\cong\\ \\mathbb R/T\\mathbb Z,\\qquad\n [c]\\longmapsto e^{-iPt},\\quad [t]\\longmapsto e^{-iPt}.\n \\tag{PS7}\n\\]\">\\[\n H^1_\\theta(\\mathbb R,A)\\ \\cong\\ \\mathbb T\n \\ \\cong\\ \\mathbb R/T\\mathbb Z,\\qquad\n [c]\\longmapsto e^{-iPt},\\quad [t]\\longmapsto e^{-iPt}.\n \\tag{PS7}\n\\]<\/span>\nIn particular the coboundaries are closed, and their quotient topology agrees with their subspace topology here. This last assertion follows also by open mapping from <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(A/\\mathbb T\\)\">\\(A/\\mathbb T\\)<\/span> onto the closed kernel.<\/p>\n<p>Write <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(E\\)\">\\(E\\)<\/span> for the normalizers in the continuous core and <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(N_c=\\operatorname{Cntr}(M)\\)\">\\(N_c=\\operatorname{Cntr}(M)\\)<\/span>. With the convention <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(\\delta(v)_s=v^*\\theta_s(v)\\)\">\\(\\delta(v)_s=v^*\\theta_s(v)\\)<\/span>, the full square is\n<span class=\"math display\" data-tex=\"\\[\n\\begin{array}{ccccc}\n \\mathbb T&amp;\\longrightarrow&amp;A&amp;\\longrightarrow&amp;B^1_\\theta\\\\\n \\downarrow&amp;&amp;\\downarrow&amp;&amp;\\downarrow\\\\\n \\mathcal U(M)&amp;\\longrightarrow&amp;E&amp;\\longrightarrow&amp;Z^1_\\theta\\\\\n \\downarrow&amp;&amp;\\downarrow&amp;&amp;\\downarrow\\\\\n \\operatorname{Int}(M)&amp;\\longrightarrow&amp;N_c&amp;\\xrightarrow{\\nu}&amp;\\mathbb R/T\\mathbb Z .\n\\end{array}\n\\tag{PS8}\n\\]\">\\[\n\\begin{array}{ccccc}\n \\mathbb T&amp;\\longrightarrow&amp;A&amp;\\longrightarrow&amp;B^1_\\theta\\\\\n \\downarrow&amp;&amp;\\downarrow&amp;&amp;\\downarrow\\\\\n \\mathcal U(M)&amp;\\longrightarrow&amp;E&amp;\\longrightarrow&amp;Z^1_\\theta\\\\\n \\downarrow&amp;&amp;\\downarrow&amp;&amp;\\downarrow\\\\\n \\operatorname{Int}(M)&amp;\\longrightarrow&amp;N_c&amp;\\xrightarrow{\\nu}&amp;\\mathbb R/T\\mathbb Z .\n\\end{array}\n\\tag{PS8}\n\\]<\/span>\nEvery row and column is exact with <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(1\\)\">\\(1\\)<\/span>'s at both ends, by the already proved general characteristic square and the actual identification (PS7). Explicitly, if <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(v\\in E\\)\">\\(v\\in E\\)<\/span>, factor its defect by (PS6):\n<span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(\\delta(v)=e^{-it\\,\\cdot}\\delta(a)\\)\">\\(\\delta(v)=e^{-it\\,\\cdot}\\delta(a)\\)<\/span>.\nThen <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(v a^*\\lambda_\\varphi(t)^*\\)\">\\(v a^*\\lambda_\\varphi(t)^*\\)<\/span> is fixed by <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(\\theta\\)\">\\(\\theta\\)<\/span>, hence belongs to <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(\\mathcal U(M)\\)\">\\(\\mathcal U(M)\\)<\/span>. Therefore\n<span class=\"math display\" data-tex=\"\\[\n N_c=\\{\\operatorname{Ad}(w)\\sigma_t^\\varphi:\n               w\\in\\mathcal U(M),\\ t\\in\\mathbb R\\},\\qquad\n \\nu(\\operatorname{Ad}(w)\\sigma_t^\\varphi)=[t].\n \\tag{PS9}\n\\]\">\\[\n N_c=\\{\\operatorname{Ad}(w)\\sigma_t^\\varphi:\n               w\\in\\mathcal U(M),\\ t\\in\\mathbb R\\},\\qquad\n \\nu(\\operatorname{Ad}(w)\\sigma_t^\\varphi)=[t].\n \\tag{PS9}\n\\]<\/span>\nThe kernel and independence of this formula follow from (PS7) and exactness, or from the actual inner-period theorem in DDP2. Thus the formula retains all extended inner automorphisms.<\/p>\n<p>The <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(\\operatorname{Aut}(M)\\times\\mathbb R\\)\">\\(\\operatorname{Aut}(M)\\times\\mathbb R\\)<\/span> action of the general square is retained. On the center, every canonical lift commutes with translation. Such an automorphism is a rotation: the eigenspace for the character <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(e^{-iTs}\\)\">\\(e^{-iTs}\\)<\/span> consists of scalar multiples of <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(e^{iTq}\\)\">\\(e^{iTq}\\)<\/span>, since the ratio of two such eigenunitaries is invariant. It therefore sends the generating unitary to a scalar multiple and hence acts by a unique translation modulo <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(P\\)\">\\(P\\)<\/span>. Scalar monodromy is unchanged. Thus both acting groups act trivially on the last entry of (PS8), although their actions on <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(A,E\\)\">\\(A,E\\)<\/span> and the automorphism groups need not be trivial.<\/p>\n","source":"docs/courses/OA-FLOW/src/OA-FLOW-PSQ.md","start_offset":2712,"end_offset":7568,"role":"registered_statement_and_proof_passage"},"9e6b2b2073a396c7cb860b5cd5b2a1a76743c436f00a488710dae057f0574ec6":{"markdown":"## 4. Projection-valued measures and the spectral theorem\n\n**Definition 4.1.** Let \\(X\\) be a metric space; we use \\(X=\\mathbb R\\) and \\(X=\\mathbb C\\). A *projection-valued measure* on \\(X\\), acting on \\(H\\), is a map \\(E\\) from the Borel sets of \\(X\\) to the projections of \\(B(H)\\) with two properties:\n- \\(E(X)=1\\);\n- for every \\(\\xi\\in H\\), the function \\(\\mu^E_\\xi(\\Delta)=\\langle E(\\Delta)\\xi,\\xi\\rangle=\\|E(\\Delta)\\xi\\|^2\\) is a measure.\n\n\\(E\\) is *supported* by a Borel set \\(Y\\) if \\(E(Y)=1\\).\n\n**Lemma 4.2.** Let \\(E\\) be a projection-valued measure.\n1. \\(E(\\varnothing)=0\\). If \\(\\Delta\\cap\\Delta'=\\varnothing\\), then \\(E(\\Delta\\cup\\Delta')=E(\\Delta)+E(\\Delta')\\) and \\(E(\\Delta)E(\\Delta')=0\\).\n2. \\(E(\\Delta\\cap\\Delta')=E(\\Delta)E(\\Delta')=E(\\Delta')E(\\Delta)\\) for all Borel sets \\(\\Delta,\\Delta'\\).\n3. If \\(\\Delta\\) is the union of disjoint Borel sets \\(\\Delta_n\\), then \\(E(\\Delta)\\xi=\\sum_nE(\\Delta_n)\\xi\\) for every \\(\\xi\\in H\\).\n\n**Proof.** (1) \\(\\|E(\\varnothing)\\xi\\|^2=\\mu^E_\\xi(\\varnothing)=0\\) for every \\(\\xi\\).\n- For disjoint \\(\\Delta,\\Delta'\\), additivity of \\(\\mu^E_\\xi\\) gives \\(\\langle E(\\Delta\\cup\\Delta')\\xi,\\xi\\rangle=\\langle(E(\\Delta)+E(\\Delta'))\\xi,\\xi\\rangle\\) for every \\(\\xi\\). So \\(E(\\Delta\\cup\\Delta')=E(\\Delta)+E(\\Delta')\\) (the Hilbert-space lesson, Corollary 3.2).\n- If \\(P\\), \\(Q\\) and \\(P+Q\\) are projections, then \\((P+Q)^2=P+Q\\) gives \\(PQ+QP=0\\). Multiplying by \\(P\\) on the left and on the right gives \\(PQ+PQP=0=PQP+QP\\). So \\(PQ=QP\\), and then \\(2PQ=0\\).\n\n(2) The sets \\(\\Delta\\cap\\Delta'\\), \\(\\Delta\\setminus\\Delta'\\) and \\(\\Delta'\\setminus\\Delta\\) are disjoint. By (1),\n\\[\n\\begin{gathered}\nE(\\Delta)\\\\\n=E(\\Delta\\cap\\Delta')+E(\\Delta\\setminus\\Delta'),\\\\\nE(\\Delta')\\\\\n=E(\\Delta\\cap\\Delta')+E(\\Delta'\\setminus\\Delta),\n\\end{gathered}\n\\]\nand the products of the projections of two disjoint sets vanish. Multiplying out gives \\(E(\\Delta)E(\\Delta')=E(\\Delta\\cap\\Delta')\\), and likewise in the other order.\n\n(3) The vectors \\(E(\\Delta_n)\\xi\\) are mutually orthogonal, by (1). Also by (1),\n\\[\n\\begin{gathered}\n\\Big\\|E(\\Delta)\\xi-\\sum_{n\\leq N}E(\\Delta_n)\\xi\\Big\\|^2\\\\\n=\\Big\\|E\\Big(\\Delta\\setminus\\bigcup_{n\\leq N}\\Delta_n\\Big)\\xi\\Big\\|^2\\\\\n=\\mu^E_\\xi\\Big(\\Delta\\setminus\\bigcup_{n\\leq N}\\Delta_n\\Big).\n\\end{gathered}\n\\]\nThis tends to \\(0\\), because \\(\\mu^E_\\xi\\) is a finite measure. \\(\\square\\)\n\n**Proposition 4.3** (Integration against a projection-valued measure). Let \\(E\\) be a projection-valued measure on \\(X\\). There is exactly one linear map \\(f\\mapsto\\int f\\,dE\\) from \\(B_b(X)\\) to \\(B(H)\\) with\n\\[\n\\begin{gathered}\n\\int1_\\Delta\\,dE\\\\\n=E(\\Delta)\\\\\n\\text{for every Borel set }\\Delta,\\\\\n\\Big\\|\\int f\\,dE\\Big\\|\\\\\n\\leq\\|f\\|_X .\n\\end{gathered}\n\\]\nIt is a unital \\(*\\)-homomorphism, and \\(\\big\\langle\\big(\\int f\\,dE\\big)\\xi,\\xi\\big\\rangle=\\int f\\,d\\mu^E_\\xi\\).\n\n**Proof.** *Simple functions.* For a Borel simple function \\(f=\\sum_jc_j1_{\\Delta_j}\\) with disjoint \\(\\Delta_j\\), put \\(\\int f\\,dE=\\sum_jc_jE(\\Delta_j)\\).\n- This does not depend on the representation: two representations with disjoint sets have a common refinement, and Lemma 4.2(1) shows that refining does not change the sum.\n- The vectors \\(E(\\Delta_j)\\xi\\) are orthogonal, and \\(\\sum_j\\|E(\\Delta_j)\\xi\\|^2=\\|E(\\bigcup_j\\Delta_j)\\xi\\|^2\\leq\\|\\xi\\|^2\\). So\n\\[\n\\begin{gathered}\n\\Big\\|\\Big(\\int f\\,dE\\Big)\\xi\\Big\\|^2\\\\\n=\\sum_j|c_j|^2\\|E(\\Delta_j)\\xi\\|^2\\\\\n\\leq\\|f\\|_X^2\\|\\xi\\|^2 .\n\\end{gathered}\n\\]\n- On simple functions the map is linear and multiplicative (Lemma 4.2(2)), it preserves the involution (each \\(E(\\Delta)\\) is self-adjoint), it is unital, and \\(\\langle(\\int f\\,dE)\\xi,\\xi\\rangle=\\sum_jc_j\\mu^E_\\xi(\\Delta_j)=\\int f\\,d\\mu^E_\\xi\\).\n\n*Extension.* The simple functions are dense in \\(B_b(X)\\) for \\(\\|\\cdot\\|_X\\) (Lemma 1.1, step (d)). A linear contraction defined on them extends uniquely to a linear contraction on \\(B_b(X)\\). The identities above pass to uniform limits, since \\(\\mu^E_\\xi\\) is finite. Any linear contraction with \\(\\int1_\\Delta\\,dE=E(\\Delta)\\) agrees with this one on simple functions, hence everywhere. \\(\\square\\)\n\n**Theorem 4.4** (Spectral theorem). Let \\(h\\in B(H)\\) be self-adjoint with spectrum \\(S\\).\n1. \\(E_h(\\Delta)=1_\\Delta(h)\\), for Borel sets \\(\\Delta\\subseteq\\mathbb R\\), is a projection-valued measure on \\(\\mathbb R\\), supported by \\(S\\subseteq[-\\|h\\|,\\|h\\|]\\). For every bounded Borel function \\(f\\) on \\(\\mathbb R\\), \\(\\int f\\,dE_h=f(h)\\). In particular\n\\[\nh=\\int\\iota_S\\,dE_h ,\n\\]\nwhere \\(\\iota_S(\\lambda)=\\lambda\\) on \\(S\\) and \\(\\iota_S=0\\) off \\(S\\). This is usually written \\(h=\\int\\lambda\\,dE_h(\\lambda)\\).\n2. (*Uniqueness.*) Let \\(E\\) be a projection-valued measure on \\(\\mathbb R\\), supported by a compact set \\(L\\), with \\(h=\\int\\iota_L\\,dE\\). Then \\(E=E_h\\).\n3. For \\(\\xi\\in H\\), the spectral measure satisfies \\(\\mu_\\xi(\\Delta)=\\langle E_h(\\Delta)\\xi,\\xi\\rangle=\\|E_h(\\Delta)\\xi\\|^2\\), \\(\\mu_\\xi(\\mathbb R)=\\|\\xi\\|^2\\), and \\(\\langle f(h)\\xi,\\xi\\rangle=\\int f\\,d\\mu_\\xi\\).\n4. Each \\(E_h(\\Delta)\\) commutes with every operator that commutes with \\(h\\).\n\n**Proof.** (1) \\(1_\\Delta\\) is real and \\(1_\\Delta^2=1_\\Delta\\), so \\(E_h(\\Delta)\\) is a projection, by Theorem 3.1(2). Also:\n- \\(E_h(\\mathbb R)=1(h)=1\\);\n- \\(\\langle E_h(\\Delta)\\xi,\\xi\\rangle=\\mu_\\xi(\\Delta\\cap S)\\) is a measure in \\(\\Delta\\);\n- \\(E_h(S)=1\\);\n- \\(S\\subseteq[-\\|h\\|,\\|h\\|]\\) (Conventions).\n\nThe maps \\(f\\mapsto\\int f\\,dE_h\\) and \\(f\\mapsto f(h)\\) are linear contractions on the bounded Borel functions on \\(\\mathbb R\\) (Proposition 4.3 and Theorem 3.1(3)). They agree on indicators, hence on simple functions, hence everywhere. For \\(f=\\iota_S\\), \\(f|_S\\) is the identity function of \\(S\\), and the continuous calculus sends it to \\(h\\).\n\n(2) Let \\(L'=L\\cup S\\), a compact set.\n- \\(E(\\mathbb R\\setminus L)=0\\), so \\(\\mu^E_\\xi\\) is carried by \\(L\\).\n- \\(\\int\\cdot\\,dE\\) is a unital homomorphism, so \\(\\int(p\\circ\\iota_L)\\,dE=p(h)\\) for every polynomial \\(p\\). Since \\(p\\circ\\iota_L=p\\) on \\(L\\),\n\\[\n\\int_Lp\\,d\\mu^E_\\xi=\\langle p(h)\\xi,\\xi\\rangle=\\int_Sp\\,d\\mu_\\xi .\n\\]\n- So the finite measures \\(\\mu^E_\\xi\\) and \\(\\mu_\\xi\\), restricted to the Borel subsets of \\(L'\\), have the same integrals of polynomials. The polynomials are uniformly dense in \\(C(L')\\) ([the Stone–Weierstrass lesson](stone-weierstrass-c0.md#oa-fnd-sw-17)), so the two measures have the same integrals of continuous functions on \\(L'\\). By Corollary 1.2 they are equal.\n- Both vanish off \\(L'\\). So \\(\\langle E(\\Delta)\\xi,\\xi\\rangle=\\mu_\\xi(\\Delta)=\\langle E_h(\\Delta)\\xi,\\xi\\rangle\\) for every \\(\\xi\\), and \\(E(\\Delta)=E_h(\\Delta)\\).\n\n(3) restates the definitions with Proposition 2.1 and (3.1). (4) is Theorem 3.1(5). \\(\\square\\)\n\n**Remark 4.5** (Exponentials). For \\(t\\in\\mathbb R\\), \\(e^{ith}\\) denotes \\(f(h)\\) with \\(f(\\lambda)=e^{it\\lambda}\\). Since \\(f\\) is continuous, this is the continuous calculus. The partial sums of \\(\\sum_n(it\\lambda)^n/n!\\) converge to \\(f\\) uniformly on \\(S\\), and the calculus is isometric. So \\(e^{ith}\\) is the sum of the norm-convergent series \\(\\sum_n(ith)^n/n!\\). It is unitary, because \\(\\bar ff=1\\).","html":"<h2>4. Projection-valued measures and the spectral theorem<\/h2>\n<p><strong>Definition 4.1.<\/strong> Let <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(X\\)\">\\(X\\)<\/span> be a metric space; we use <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(X=\\mathbb R\\)\">\\(X=\\mathbb R\\)<\/span> and <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(X=\\mathbb C\\)\">\\(X=\\mathbb C\\)<\/span>. A <em>projection-valued measure<\/em> on <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(X\\)\">\\(X\\)<\/span>, acting on <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(H\\)\">\\(H\\)<\/span>, is a map <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(E\\)\">\\(E\\)<\/span> from the Borel sets of <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(X\\)\">\\(X\\)<\/span> to the projections of <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(B(H)\\)\">\\(B(H)\\)<\/span> with two properties:<\/p>\n<ul>\n<li><span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(E(X)=1\\)\">\\(E(X)=1\\)<\/span>;<\/li>\n<li>for every <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(\\xi\\in H\\)\">\\(\\xi\\in H\\)<\/span>, the function <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(\\mu^E_\\xi(\\Delta)=\\langle E(\\Delta)\\xi,\\xi\\rangle=\\|E(\\Delta)\\xi\\|^2\\)\">\\(\\mu^E_\\xi(\\Delta)=\\langle E(\\Delta)\\xi,\\xi\\rangle=\\|E(\\Delta)\\xi\\|^2\\)<\/span> is a measure.<\/li>\n<\/ul>\n<p><span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(E\\)\">\\(E\\)<\/span> is <em>supported<\/em> by a Borel set <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(Y\\)\">\\(Y\\)<\/span> if <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(E(Y)=1\\)\">\\(E(Y)=1\\)<\/span>.<\/p>\n<p><strong>Lemma 4.2.<\/strong> Let <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(E\\)\">\\(E\\)<\/span> be a projection-valued measure.<\/p>\n<ol>\n<li><span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(E(\\varnothing)=0\\)\">\\(E(\\varnothing)=0\\)<\/span>. If <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(\\Delta\\cap\\Delta&#x27;=\\varnothing\\)\">\\(\\Delta\\cap\\Delta&#x27;=\\varnothing\\)<\/span>, then <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(E(\\Delta\\cup\\Delta&#x27;)=E(\\Delta)+E(\\Delta&#x27;)\\)\">\\(E(\\Delta\\cup\\Delta&#x27;)=E(\\Delta)+E(\\Delta&#x27;)\\)<\/span> and <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(E(\\Delta)E(\\Delta&#x27;)=0\\)\">\\(E(\\Delta)E(\\Delta&#x27;)=0\\)<\/span>.<\/li>\n<li><span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(E(\\Delta\\cap\\Delta&#x27;)=E(\\Delta)E(\\Delta&#x27;)=E(\\Delta&#x27;)E(\\Delta)\\)\">\\(E(\\Delta\\cap\\Delta&#x27;)=E(\\Delta)E(\\Delta&#x27;)=E(\\Delta&#x27;)E(\\Delta)\\)<\/span> for all Borel sets <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(\\Delta,\\Delta&#x27;\\)\">\\(\\Delta,\\Delta&#x27;\\)<\/span>.<\/li>\n<li>If <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(\\Delta\\)\">\\(\\Delta\\)<\/span> is the union of disjoint Borel sets <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(\\Delta_n\\)\">\\(\\Delta_n\\)<\/span>, then <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(E(\\Delta)\\xi=\\sum_nE(\\Delta_n)\\xi\\)\">\\(E(\\Delta)\\xi=\\sum_nE(\\Delta_n)\\xi\\)<\/span> for every <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(\\xi\\in H\\)\">\\(\\xi\\in H\\)<\/span>.<\/li>\n<\/ol>\n<p><strong>Proof.<\/strong> (1) <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(\\|E(\\varnothing)\\xi\\|^2=\\mu^E_\\xi(\\varnothing)=0\\)\">\\(\\|E(\\varnothing)\\xi\\|^2=\\mu^E_\\xi(\\varnothing)=0\\)<\/span> for every <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(\\xi\\)\">\\(\\xi\\)<\/span>.<\/p>\n<ul>\n<li>For disjoint <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(\\Delta,\\Delta&#x27;\\)\">\\(\\Delta,\\Delta&#x27;\\)<\/span>, additivity of <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(\\mu^E_\\xi\\)\">\\(\\mu^E_\\xi\\)<\/span> gives <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(\\langle E(\\Delta\\cup\\Delta&#x27;)\\xi,\\xi\\rangle=\\langle(E(\\Delta)+E(\\Delta&#x27;))\\xi,\\xi\\rangle\\)\">\\(\\langle E(\\Delta\\cup\\Delta&#x27;)\\xi,\\xi\\rangle=\\langle(E(\\Delta)+E(\\Delta&#x27;))\\xi,\\xi\\rangle\\)<\/span> for every <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(\\xi\\)\">\\(\\xi\\)<\/span>. So <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(E(\\Delta\\cup\\Delta&#x27;)=E(\\Delta)+E(\\Delta&#x27;)\\)\">\\(E(\\Delta\\cup\\Delta&#x27;)=E(\\Delta)+E(\\Delta&#x27;)\\)<\/span> (the Hilbert-space lesson, Corollary 3.2).<\/li>\n<li>If <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(P\\)\">\\(P\\)<\/span>, <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(Q\\)\">\\(Q\\)<\/span> and <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(P+Q\\)\">\\(P+Q\\)<\/span> are projections, then <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\((P+Q)^2=P+Q\\)\">\\((P+Q)^2=P+Q\\)<\/span> gives <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(PQ+QP=0\\)\">\\(PQ+QP=0\\)<\/span>. Multiplying by <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(P\\)\">\\(P\\)<\/span> on the left and on the right gives <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(PQ+PQP=0=PQP+QP\\)\">\\(PQ+PQP=0=PQP+QP\\)<\/span>. So <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(PQ=QP\\)\">\\(PQ=QP\\)<\/span>, and then <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(2PQ=0\\)\">\\(2PQ=0\\)<\/span>.<\/li>\n<\/ul>\n<p>(2) The sets <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(\\Delta\\cap\\Delta&#x27;\\)\">\\(\\Delta\\cap\\Delta&#x27;\\)<\/span>, <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(\\Delta\\setminus\\Delta&#x27;\\)\">\\(\\Delta\\setminus\\Delta&#x27;\\)<\/span> and <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(\\Delta&#x27;\\setminus\\Delta\\)\">\\(\\Delta&#x27;\\setminus\\Delta\\)<\/span> are disjoint. By (1),\n<span class=\"math display\" data-tex=\"\\[\n\\begin{gathered}\nE(\\Delta)\\\\\n=E(\\Delta\\cap\\Delta&#x27;)+E(\\Delta\\setminus\\Delta&#x27;),\\\\\nE(\\Delta&#x27;)\\\\\n=E(\\Delta\\cap\\Delta&#x27;)+E(\\Delta&#x27;\\setminus\\Delta),\n\\end{gathered}\n\\]\">\\[\n\\begin{gathered}\nE(\\Delta)\\\\\n=E(\\Delta\\cap\\Delta&#x27;)+E(\\Delta\\setminus\\Delta&#x27;),\\\\\nE(\\Delta&#x27;)\\\\\n=E(\\Delta\\cap\\Delta&#x27;)+E(\\Delta&#x27;\\setminus\\Delta),\n\\end{gathered}\n\\]<\/span>\nand the products of the projections of two disjoint sets vanish. Multiplying out gives <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(E(\\Delta)E(\\Delta&#x27;)=E(\\Delta\\cap\\Delta&#x27;)\\)\">\\(E(\\Delta)E(\\Delta&#x27;)=E(\\Delta\\cap\\Delta&#x27;)\\)<\/span>, and likewise in the other order.<\/p>\n<p>(3) The vectors <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(E(\\Delta_n)\\xi\\)\">\\(E(\\Delta_n)\\xi\\)<\/span> are mutually orthogonal, by (1). Also by (1),\n<span class=\"math display\" data-tex=\"\\[\n\\begin{gathered}\n\\Big\\|E(\\Delta)\\xi-\\sum_{n\\leq N}E(\\Delta_n)\\xi\\Big\\|^2\\\\\n=\\Big\\|E\\Big(\\Delta\\setminus\\bigcup_{n\\leq N}\\Delta_n\\Big)\\xi\\Big\\|^2\\\\\n=\\mu^E_\\xi\\Big(\\Delta\\setminus\\bigcup_{n\\leq N}\\Delta_n\\Big).\n\\end{gathered}\n\\]\">\\[\n\\begin{gathered}\n\\Big\\|E(\\Delta)\\xi-\\sum_{n\\leq N}E(\\Delta_n)\\xi\\Big\\|^2\\\\\n=\\Big\\|E\\Big(\\Delta\\setminus\\bigcup_{n\\leq N}\\Delta_n\\Big)\\xi\\Big\\|^2\\\\\n=\\mu^E_\\xi\\Big(\\Delta\\setminus\\bigcup_{n\\leq N}\\Delta_n\\Big).\n\\end{gathered}\n\\]<\/span>\nThis tends to <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(0\\)\">\\(0\\)<\/span>, because <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(\\mu^E_\\xi\\)\">\\(\\mu^E_\\xi\\)<\/span> is a finite measure. <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(\\square\\)\">\\(\\square\\)<\/span><\/p>\n<p><strong>Proposition 4.3<\/strong> (Integration against a projection-valued measure). Let <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(E\\)\">\\(E\\)<\/span> be a projection-valued measure on <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(X\\)\">\\(X\\)<\/span>. There is exactly one linear map <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(f\\mapsto\\int f\\,dE\\)\">\\(f\\mapsto\\int f\\,dE\\)<\/span> from <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(B_b(X)\\)\">\\(B_b(X)\\)<\/span> to <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(B(H)\\)\">\\(B(H)\\)<\/span> with\n<span class=\"math display\" data-tex=\"\\[\n\\begin{gathered}\n\\int1_\\Delta\\,dE\\\\\n=E(\\Delta)\\\\\n\\text{for every Borel set }\\Delta,\\\\\n\\Big\\|\\int f\\,dE\\Big\\|\\\\\n\\leq\\|f\\|_X .\n\\end{gathered}\n\\]\">\\[\n\\begin{gathered}\n\\int1_\\Delta\\,dE\\\\\n=E(\\Delta)\\\\\n\\text{for every Borel set }\\Delta,\\\\\n\\Big\\|\\int f\\,dE\\Big\\|\\\\\n\\leq\\|f\\|_X .\n\\end{gathered}\n\\]<\/span>\nIt is a unital <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(*\\)\">\\(*\\)<\/span>-homomorphism, and <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(\\big\\langle\\big(\\int f\\,dE\\big)\\xi,\\xi\\big\\rangle=\\int f\\,d\\mu^E_\\xi\\)\">\\(\\big\\langle\\big(\\int f\\,dE\\big)\\xi,\\xi\\big\\rangle=\\int f\\,d\\mu^E_\\xi\\)<\/span>.<\/p>\n<p><strong>Proof.<\/strong> <em>Simple functions.<\/em> For a Borel simple function <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(f=\\sum_jc_j1_{\\Delta_j}\\)\">\\(f=\\sum_jc_j1_{\\Delta_j}\\)<\/span> with disjoint <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(\\Delta_j\\)\">\\(\\Delta_j\\)<\/span>, put <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(\\int f\\,dE=\\sum_jc_jE(\\Delta_j)\\)\">\\(\\int f\\,dE=\\sum_jc_jE(\\Delta_j)\\)<\/span>.<\/p>\n<ul>\n<li>This does not depend on the representation: two representations with disjoint sets have a common refinement, and Lemma 4.2(1) shows that refining does not change the sum.<\/li>\n<li>The vectors <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(E(\\Delta_j)\\xi\\)\">\\(E(\\Delta_j)\\xi\\)<\/span> are orthogonal, and <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(\\sum_j\\|E(\\Delta_j)\\xi\\|^2=\\|E(\\bigcup_j\\Delta_j)\\xi\\|^2\\leq\\|\\xi\\|^2\\)\">\\(\\sum_j\\|E(\\Delta_j)\\xi\\|^2=\\|E(\\bigcup_j\\Delta_j)\\xi\\|^2\\leq\\|\\xi\\|^2\\)<\/span>. So\n<span class=\"math display\" data-tex=\"\\[\n\\begin{gathered}\n\\Big\\|\\Big(\\int f\\,dE\\Big)\\xi\\Big\\|^2\\\\\n=\\sum_j|c_j|^2\\|E(\\Delta_j)\\xi\\|^2\\\\\n\\leq\\|f\\|_X^2\\|\\xi\\|^2 .\n\\end{gathered}\n\\]\">\\[\n\\begin{gathered}\n\\Big\\|\\Big(\\int f\\,dE\\Big)\\xi\\Big\\|^2\\\\\n=\\sum_j|c_j|^2\\|E(\\Delta_j)\\xi\\|^2\\\\\n\\leq\\|f\\|_X^2\\|\\xi\\|^2 .\n\\end{gathered}\n\\]<\/span><\/li>\n<li>On simple functions the map is linear and multiplicative (Lemma 4.2(2)), it preserves the involution (each <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(E(\\Delta)\\)\">\\(E(\\Delta)\\)<\/span> is self-adjoint), it is unital, and <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(\\langle(\\int f\\,dE)\\xi,\\xi\\rangle=\\sum_jc_j\\mu^E_\\xi(\\Delta_j)=\\int f\\,d\\mu^E_\\xi\\)\">\\(\\langle(\\int f\\,dE)\\xi,\\xi\\rangle=\\sum_jc_j\\mu^E_\\xi(\\Delta_j)=\\int f\\,d\\mu^E_\\xi\\)<\/span>.<\/li>\n<\/ul>\n<p><em>Extension.<\/em> The simple functions are dense in <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(B_b(X)\\)\">\\(B_b(X)\\)<\/span> for <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(\\|\\cdot\\|_X\\)\">\\(\\|\\cdot\\|_X\\)<\/span> (Lemma 1.1, step (d)). A linear contraction defined on them extends uniquely to a linear contraction on <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(B_b(X)\\)\">\\(B_b(X)\\)<\/span>. The identities above pass to uniform limits, since <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(\\mu^E_\\xi\\)\">\\(\\mu^E_\\xi\\)<\/span> is finite. Any linear contraction with <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(\\int1_\\Delta\\,dE=E(\\Delta)\\)\">\\(\\int1_\\Delta\\,dE=E(\\Delta)\\)<\/span> agrees with this one on simple functions, hence everywhere. <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(\\square\\)\">\\(\\square\\)<\/span><\/p>\n<p><strong>Theorem 4.4<\/strong> (Spectral theorem). Let <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(h\\in B(H)\\)\">\\(h\\in B(H)\\)<\/span> be self-adjoint with spectrum <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(S\\)\">\\(S\\)<\/span>.<\/p>\n<ol>\n<li><span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(E_h(\\Delta)=1_\\Delta(h)\\)\">\\(E_h(\\Delta)=1_\\Delta(h)\\)<\/span>, for Borel sets <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(\\Delta\\subseteq\\mathbb R\\)\">\\(\\Delta\\subseteq\\mathbb R\\)<\/span>, is a projection-valued measure on <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(\\mathbb R\\)\">\\(\\mathbb R\\)<\/span>, supported by <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(S\\subseteq[-\\|h\\|,\\|h\\|]\\)\">\\(S\\subseteq[-\\|h\\|,\\|h\\|]\\)<\/span>. For every bounded Borel function <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(f\\)\">\\(f\\)<\/span> on <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(\\mathbb R\\)\">\\(\\mathbb R\\)<\/span>, <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(\\int f\\,dE_h=f(h)\\)\">\\(\\int f\\,dE_h=f(h)\\)<\/span>. In particular\n<span class=\"math display\" data-tex=\"\\[\nh=\\int\\iota_S\\,dE_h ,\n\\]\">\\[\nh=\\int\\iota_S\\,dE_h ,\n\\]<\/span>\nwhere <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(\\iota_S(\\lambda)=\\lambda\\)\">\\(\\iota_S(\\lambda)=\\lambda\\)<\/span> on <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(S\\)\">\\(S\\)<\/span> and <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(\\iota_S=0\\)\">\\(\\iota_S=0\\)<\/span> off <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(S\\)\">\\(S\\)<\/span>. This is usually written <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(h=\\int\\lambda\\,dE_h(\\lambda)\\)\">\\(h=\\int\\lambda\\,dE_h(\\lambda)\\)<\/span>.<\/li>\n<li>(<em>Uniqueness.<\/em>) Let <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(E\\)\">\\(E\\)<\/span> be a projection-valued measure on <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(\\mathbb R\\)\">\\(\\mathbb R\\)<\/span>, supported by a compact set <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(L\\)\">\\(L\\)<\/span>, with <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(h=\\int\\iota_L\\,dE\\)\">\\(h=\\int\\iota_L\\,dE\\)<\/span>. Then <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(E=E_h\\)\">\\(E=E_h\\)<\/span>.<\/li>\n<li>For <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(\\xi\\in H\\)\">\\(\\xi\\in H\\)<\/span>, the spectral measure satisfies <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(\\mu_\\xi(\\Delta)=\\langle E_h(\\Delta)\\xi,\\xi\\rangle=\\|E_h(\\Delta)\\xi\\|^2\\)\">\\(\\mu_\\xi(\\Delta)=\\langle E_h(\\Delta)\\xi,\\xi\\rangle=\\|E_h(\\Delta)\\xi\\|^2\\)<\/span>, <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(\\mu_\\xi(\\mathbb R)=\\|\\xi\\|^2\\)\">\\(\\mu_\\xi(\\mathbb R)=\\|\\xi\\|^2\\)<\/span>, and <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(\\langle f(h)\\xi,\\xi\\rangle=\\int f\\,d\\mu_\\xi\\)\">\\(\\langle f(h)\\xi,\\xi\\rangle=\\int f\\,d\\mu_\\xi\\)<\/span>.<\/li>\n<li>Each <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(E_h(\\Delta)\\)\">\\(E_h(\\Delta)\\)<\/span> commutes with every operator that commutes with <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(h\\)\">\\(h\\)<\/span>.<\/li>\n<\/ol>\n<p><strong>Proof.<\/strong> (1) <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(1_\\Delta\\)\">\\(1_\\Delta\\)<\/span> is real and <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(1_\\Delta^2=1_\\Delta\\)\">\\(1_\\Delta^2=1_\\Delta\\)<\/span>, so <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(E_h(\\Delta)\\)\">\\(E_h(\\Delta)\\)<\/span> is a projection, by Theorem 3.1(2). Also:<\/p>\n<ul>\n<li><span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(E_h(\\mathbb R)=1(h)=1\\)\">\\(E_h(\\mathbb R)=1(h)=1\\)<\/span>;<\/li>\n<li><span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(\\langle E_h(\\Delta)\\xi,\\xi\\rangle=\\mu_\\xi(\\Delta\\cap S)\\)\">\\(\\langle E_h(\\Delta)\\xi,\\xi\\rangle=\\mu_\\xi(\\Delta\\cap S)\\)<\/span> is a measure in <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(\\Delta\\)\">\\(\\Delta\\)<\/span>;<\/li>\n<li><span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(E_h(S)=1\\)\">\\(E_h(S)=1\\)<\/span>;<\/li>\n<li><span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(S\\subseteq[-\\|h\\|,\\|h\\|]\\)\">\\(S\\subseteq[-\\|h\\|,\\|h\\|]\\)<\/span> (Conventions).<\/li>\n<\/ul>\n<p>The maps <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(f\\mapsto\\int f\\,dE_h\\)\">\\(f\\mapsto\\int f\\,dE_h\\)<\/span> and <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(f\\mapsto f(h)\\)\">\\(f\\mapsto f(h)\\)<\/span> are linear contractions on the bounded Borel functions on <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(\\mathbb R\\)\">\\(\\mathbb R\\)<\/span> (Proposition 4.3 and Theorem 3.1(3)). They agree on indicators, hence on simple functions, hence everywhere. For <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(f=\\iota_S\\)\">\\(f=\\iota_S\\)<\/span>, <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(f|_S\\)\">\\(f|_S\\)<\/span> is the identity function of <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(S\\)\">\\(S\\)<\/span>, and the continuous calculus sends it to <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(h\\)\">\\(h\\)<\/span>.<\/p>\n<p>(2) Let <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(L&#x27;=L\\cup S\\)\">\\(L&#x27;=L\\cup S\\)<\/span>, a compact set.<\/p>\n<ul>\n<li><span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(E(\\mathbb R\\setminus L)=0\\)\">\\(E(\\mathbb R\\setminus L)=0\\)<\/span>, so <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(\\mu^E_\\xi\\)\">\\(\\mu^E_\\xi\\)<\/span> is carried by <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(L\\)\">\\(L\\)<\/span>.<\/li>\n<li><span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(\\int\\cdot\\,dE\\)\">\\(\\int\\cdot\\,dE\\)<\/span> is a unital homomorphism, so <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(\\int(p\\circ\\iota_L)\\,dE=p(h)\\)\">\\(\\int(p\\circ\\iota_L)\\,dE=p(h)\\)<\/span> for every polynomial <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(p\\)\">\\(p\\)<\/span>. Since <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(p\\circ\\iota_L=p\\)\">\\(p\\circ\\iota_L=p\\)<\/span> on <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(L\\)\">\\(L\\)<\/span>,\n<span class=\"math display\" data-tex=\"\\[\n\\int_Lp\\,d\\mu^E_\\xi=\\langle p(h)\\xi,\\xi\\rangle=\\int_Sp\\,d\\mu_\\xi .\n\\]\">\\[\n\\int_Lp\\,d\\mu^E_\\xi=\\langle p(h)\\xi,\\xi\\rangle=\\int_Sp\\,d\\mu_\\xi .\n\\]<\/span><\/li>\n<li>So the finite measures <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(\\mu^E_\\xi\\)\">\\(\\mu^E_\\xi\\)<\/span> and <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(\\mu_\\xi\\)\">\\(\\mu_\\xi\\)<\/span>, restricted to the Borel subsets of <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(L&#x27;\\)\">\\(L&#x27;\\)<\/span>, have the same integrals of polynomials. The polynomials are uniformly dense in <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(C(L&#x27;)\\)\">\\(C(L&#x27;)\\)<\/span> (<a title=\"Reference location unresolved in this edition\" data-unresolved-reference=\"stone-weierstrass-c0.md#oa-fnd-sw-17\">the Stone–Weierstrass lesson<\/a>), so the two measures have the same integrals of continuous functions on <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(L&#x27;\\)\">\\(L&#x27;\\)<\/span>. By Corollary 1.2 they are equal.<\/li>\n<li>Both vanish off <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(L&#x27;\\)\">\\(L&#x27;\\)<\/span>. So <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(\\langle E(\\Delta)\\xi,\\xi\\rangle=\\mu_\\xi(\\Delta)=\\langle E_h(\\Delta)\\xi,\\xi\\rangle\\)\">\\(\\langle E(\\Delta)\\xi,\\xi\\rangle=\\mu_\\xi(\\Delta)=\\langle E_h(\\Delta)\\xi,\\xi\\rangle\\)<\/span> for every <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(\\xi\\)\">\\(\\xi\\)<\/span>, and <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(E(\\Delta)=E_h(\\Delta)\\)\">\\(E(\\Delta)=E_h(\\Delta)\\)<\/span>.<\/li>\n<\/ul>\n<p>(3) restates the definitions with Proposition 2.1 and (3.1). (4) is Theorem 3.1(5). <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(\\square\\)\">\\(\\square\\)<\/span><\/p>\n<p><strong>Remark 4.5<\/strong> (Exponentials). For <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(t\\in\\mathbb R\\)\">\\(t\\in\\mathbb R\\)<\/span>, <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(e^{ith}\\)\">\\(e^{ith}\\)<\/span> denotes <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(f(h)\\)\">\\(f(h)\\)<\/span> with <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(f(\\lambda)=e^{it\\lambda}\\)\">\\(f(\\lambda)=e^{it\\lambda}\\)<\/span>. Since <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(f\\)\">\\(f\\)<\/span> is continuous, this is the continuous calculus. The partial sums of <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(\\sum_n(it\\lambda)^n/n!\\)\">\\(\\sum_n(it\\lambda)^n/n!\\)<\/span> converge to <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(f\\)\">\\(f\\)<\/span> uniformly on <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(S\\)\">\\(S\\)<\/span>, and the calculus is isometric. So <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(e^{ith}\\)\">\\(e^{ith}\\)<\/span> is the sum of the norm-convergent series <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(\\sum_n(ith)^n/n!\\)\">\\(\\sum_n(ith)^n/n!\\)<\/span>. It is unitary, because <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(\\bar ff=1\\)\">\\(\\bar ff=1\\)<\/span>.<\/p>\n","source":"docs/courses/foundations-of-von-neumann-algebras/src/the-spectral-theorem-for-bounded-self-adjoint-operators.md","start_offset":15658,"end_offset":22709,"role":"registered_statement_and_proof_passage"}});
